**Net: 23-30 weeks, up from 20-26, and 26.5-35 since X4a was approved on 5 September 2026.** Each increase is real scope, never a re-estimate. Do not let
a summary present it as the same programme.

### Five findings from that pass that generalise

- **A trading-shaped service does not fail when a second game arrives - it keeps working
  and keeps being wrong.** `lib/services/matchmaking.service.ts` and
  `GET /api/matchmaking` already exist and rank opponents by *trading* skill. Add a second
  game and it returns matches happily, all of them trading matches, with no error, no
  empty state and no log line. Same shape as the mirror-drift and `canEnterChallenges`
  defects: **the system reports success while doing the wrong thing.** The rule:
  **change the service, do not call it from a new place**, and prove it with a test that
  fails first. Risk **X13**. The same trap sits in
  `components/dashboard/GettingStartedCard.tsx`, whose steps tell every new player to
  "place your first trade".
- **"Like payment providers" was right about the UX and wrong about the storage.** The
  owner asked for games to be added the way payment providers are, and
  `PaymentProvidersSection.tsx` is indeed the right interaction model. But
  `payment-provider.model.ts` embeds `credentials[]` in the document every screen reads and
  carries a `saveToEnv` flag with a `regenerate-env` route that writes secrets to `.env`.
  `04` s3.1 deliberately keeps game credentials **out** of `game_provider`. Copying the
  pattern wholesale would have undone that on consistency grounds. **Copy the UX, not the
  persistence** - `12` s4.1.
- **A menu group is not a tab.** `12` s1 already grouped the trading sections, but the
  owner asked for one destination with tabs inside. Two things must survive the collapse:
  **deep links** (tab state must be addressable, not `useState`, or every bookmark and
  wiki screenshot breaks), and **RBAC** (`ADMIN_SECTIONS` grants per *section*, so merging
  six into one is a silent privilege widening that looks completely correct on review).
  **Keep the six IDs and gate the tabs.** `12` s1.1.
- **No aggregate may silently mean "trading only".** Now a binding rule in `05` s10: every
  performance or money figure is either generalised across games, explicitly scoped and
  labelled to one game, or removed from the platform-wide view. There is no third option.
  **"Total PnL" is the clearest casualty** - a chess puzzle has no profit and loss, so it
  moves inside the trading card or becomes something genuinely cross-game. The failure mode
  is not a crash: a trading-only total keeps computing and keeps rendering.
- **Inference is not consent.** Paying to enter a competition says a player will *play* a
  game; it does not say they want 1v1 invitations from strangers. `20` s3.2 keeps them
  separate - inferred interest drives **suggestions**, an explicit opt-in drives
  **invitations**. Conflating them turns matchmaking into spam. Risk **X14**. The same
  distinction made `canEnterChallenges` a live defect: **a stored value and an absent one
  are different facts.**

**Stage 0 is a prerequisite in both scenarios.** It fixes live defects under the money
layer, and nothing changes that.

**Stage 0 started on 1 September 2026** and is tracked in `New games plan/PROGRESS.md`,
which is the file to read before touching any of this. Three things from it are easy to
get wrong and are worth stating here:

- **Prerequisite A already shipped** (commit `d5d3a328`): the `/api/simulator/*` routes
  accepted a plain `X-Simulator-Mode: true` header as authentication in production, so an
  unauthenticated caller could credit any wallet. Recorded as **R27** in
  `External game plans/17-risk-register.md` because the provider callback specified in
  `01`/`06` can repeat the same mistake. Two rules follow: **an authentication helper must
  never accept a request because configuration is missing**, and **test the route, not just
  the helper.**
- **The finalize-time prize-pool safeguard does not mask the join-path defect.** It only
 fires when the pool is too *high*. An under-counted pool is under-distributed silently.
 Several documents used to claim the opposite; if you find that claim, it is stale.
- **Model mirror drift is a WRITE-side defect, with one read-side trap.** Measured against
 a real MongoDB on 1 Sep 2026 (`__tests__/helpers/mirror-drift-behaviour.test.ts`, 8
 tests). An ordinary `save()` does **not** strip fields the schema lacks, and `.lean()` /
 `toObject()` do **not** hide them — several documents used to claim both, and both are
 stale. What actually happens: a **missing enum value rejects the whole write**, and the
 app with the narrower copy **cannot write the field at all**, silently, while reporting
 success. **The exception, and the subtlest part: ordinary `doc.field` access returns
 `undefined`**, because Mongoose defines getters only for declared paths — so the field
 survives a debug dump while the code beside it reads nothing and takes the wrong branch.
 Two rules follow: **compare enum values, not just field names**, and **never cite a read
 as evidence that two copies agree.**
- **Defect 2 is built** (1 Sep 2026, awaiting owner test): the guard in
 `tools/model-mirror/`, all 11 drifted pairs synced, 20 tests, and the
 `mongodb-memory-server` replica-set harness the money tests need. It found **three** live
 production bugs on the way — failed withdrawals recording no time or reason, six
 landing-page sections that could not be edited at all, and hero/branding images that
 could never be restored after a redeploy. The third was found by **diffing the typecheck
 against a pre-sync baseline** (admin app 229 → 225 errors); errors that *disappear* after
 a sync mark code that was already reaching for a field its schema denied it. Worth
 repeating whenever models are synced.
- **The order is Defect 2 before Defect 1**, so the mirror guard exists before Defect 1
 edits the admin copy of the entry path.
- **Three owner decisions were taken on 1 Sep 2026** and must not be quietly reversed:
 the test database is **`mongodb-memory-server` as a single-node replica set** (built and
 proven with a real transaction); the orphaned declaration files are **deleted**; and
 **joining a competition is allowed outside market hours — only trading itself is gated.**
 That last one changes the Defect 1 design: the unified `contest-entry.service.ts` takes
 the union of the *security* checks from both gates and deliberately does **not** carry
 Gate B's market-hours check, because doing so would block weekend sign-ups for Monday
 contests. Locked in by Defect 1 test 12, which fails both a fix that blocks the join and
 one that lets the order through.
- **`apps/admin` has its own `node_modules/mongoose`.** Admin models therefore run on a
 **separate Mongoose instance** which a test's `mongoose.connect` does not touch. The
 symptom is `Operation "x.insertOne()" buffering timed out after 10000ms`, which reads like
 a slow database rather than an unconnected one. Connect it via **`Model.base`**, not a
 guessed path — that is the instance the model registered on, and it survives npm hoisting.
 Second rule from the same area: **never import both copies of one model into a single
 test.** Both register under the same name via `models.X || model(...)`, so the second
 import silently returns the first and the test examines the wrong schema while looking
 correct. **Superseded for vitest since 5 Sep 2026, and only for vitest:** `vitest.config.ts`
 sets `resolve.dedupe: ["mongoose"]`, so there is one instance and `Model.base` is moot in
 tests. That change is what made admin **actions** testable at all rather than only greppable,
 and it is required rather than convenient — a session belongs to a `MongoClient`, so
 connecting the second instance to the same URI still could not share one transaction. The
 bullet above remains true of the running apps, and do not remove the dedupe to "clean up"
 the config: the symptom returns as a 10-second buffering timeout that reads like a slow
 database.
- **A synced schema is not a working feature — the `brandingFiles` bug proved it twice.**
 Found and **fixed** 1 Sep 2026. The upload routes key the map by filename and **Mongoose
 refuses map keys containing a dot**. The sync changed the failure mode without removing it:
 before, the undeclared field meant a plain JavaScript `Map` accepted the key and the write
 was silently discarded; after, `default: new Map()` hands the route a `MongooseMap` that
 validates and throws, and the route catches it and warns while still reporting success.
 **No entry had ever been stored, so there was nothing to migrate.** Fixed by
 `lib/utils/branding-file-key.ts` (mirrored into `apps/admin`), encoding `.` as `__DOT__`,
 wired through **two writers, four readers and one delete** across both apps. Two rules
 follow: **the mirror guard proves two copies agree, not that either one works** — follow a
 drift fix with a test that round-trips real data; and **assert the round trip through the
 real helper, never a hard-coded encoded string**, or a reader that forgot to encode still
 passes.
- **Every test that recorded a defect was flipped to prove its fix, not deleted** (1 Sep
 2026, three bugs). Keep doing this. The comment that explained why the defect was reachable
 is the most valuable part of the test, and deleting it loses the reason the guard exists.
 Rename the test, invert the assertion, keep the history in the `// Reason:` block.
- **Two fixes deliberately stopped short, and both are recorded rather than silently
 scoped.** `cancelCompetitionAndRefund`'s lock is `status !== "cancelled"`, so a **completed**
 competition is still refundable — entry fees returned on top of prizes already paid. And
 `error instanceof Error ? error.message` in a route response appears **166 times across 119
 API route files**; only the proven one was changed. In both cases the wider change is a
 policy decision or an unreviewable diff, so it belongs with the unified service. **Do not
 let a summary imply either is closed.**
- **Setting the final status up front IS the lock.** The double-refund fix needed no new enum
 value: `findOneAndUpdate({ _id, status: { $ne: "cancelled" } }, { $set: { status:
 "cancelled", ... } })` claims and completes in one instruction, and a transaction abort rolls
 it back for a retry. An intermediate `cancelling` state would have meant a schema change in
 both apps for no gain. Also: **a duplicate money request should return success with a zero
 count**, not an error — a retried cron delivery has done nothing wrong, and a failure invites
 a third attempt.
- **Finalization recomputes participant stats from trades before ranking.** Seeding `pnl` on a
 `CompetitionParticipant` does nothing; it is overwritten with zero, every player ties at rank
 1, and the resulting equal payout **reads as a prize-distribution bug in production**. To
 rank participants, seed a **closed `TradingPosition` and a matching `TradeHistory` row** —
 the realized amount is read from the history by `positionId`, not derived from the position.
 Use **USD-quoted symbols** or finalization fetches conversion rates over the network, and
 satisfy the **whole** competition schema (`createdBy`, `registrationDeadline`, `description`),
 because finalization saves the document even though those fields play no part in a payout.
- **The challenge accept route was missing the restriction and fraud guards** (proven and
 then **fixed** 1 Sep 2026, `__tests__/services/challenge-accept-guards.test.ts`,
 sub-defect 1b). It checked seven other guards. The challenge **create** route checks both,
 so only accept was affected — which is why reading one route would never have found it. A
 challenge is the easiest shape for coordinated entry, being exactly two players with the
 pot returning to the pair minus the platform fee, so the fraud gap mattered here at least
 as much as on a competition. See the fix note below.
- **Classify concurrency failures, never merely count them.** Asserting that the artifact
 and unknown buckets are *empty* is what stops test-server noise inflating a finding. And
 note the trap in the other direction: a genuine write conflict reads `Write conflict during
 plan execution **and yielding is disabled**`, so matching "yielding is disabled" as an
 infrastructure marker files every real failure as noise and makes the defect vanish.
- **`ensureCollections()` alone is not enough — indexes are a catalog change too.**
 Mongoose builds a model's indexes on first use, which produces the same `due to catalog
 changes` failure inside a transaction. That residue made the sequential control test pass
 alone and fail about **one full-suite run in three**. `settleIndexes()` awaits
 `Model.init()` for every registered model and `ensureCollections()` calls it. A test that
 fails only when the suite is busy teaches the team to rerun rather than to read.
- **Before writing any concurrency test, call `ensureCollections()`** from
 `__tests__/helpers/mongo-test-server.ts` with every collection the code under test writes.
 MongoDB **cannot create a collection inside a transaction**, so the first test to touch a
 new collection fails with `due to catalog changes` — which is indistinguishable at a glance
 from a contention failure. This nearly put a false finding on record: a 20-way join test
 reported 1 success in 20, and most of the failures were this artifact. Measured after that
 artifact was removed, Gate A admitted **1 concurrent join in 20** because it had no retry
 while Gate B retried five times; **since the unified service gave both gates the retry, the
 same test measures 20 in 20.** The ratio is not a production prediction either way, because
 a single-node in-memory replica set has tighter lock timeouts. Assert against the *observed*
 success count, never against the attempt count, so the test survives the fix.
- **Test fixtures must use ObjectId-shaped user ids** (24 hex chars), not readable labels.
 Better Auth uses the MongoDB adapter so real ids are ObjectId strings; most models type
 `userId` as `String` and would accept anything, but the fraud models declare
 `Schema.Types.ObjectId` and throw a `CastError` that the entry path catches and logs. The
 test then passes while a whole branch of the code under test never runs — coverage that
 only looks like coverage.
- **Testing a server action needs `__tests__/helpers/server-action-context.ts`**, which
 supplies the session, headers, restriction and fraud outcomes that ambient request state
 would normally provide. Two things will waste your time otherwise. Server actions
 **return** `{ success: false, error }` rather than throwing, so assert on the result, not
 with `rejects.toThrow`. And they re-throw only errors whose **`digest` starts with
 `NEXT_`** — a `redirect()` mock without a digest is swallowed into a result object, the
 redirect appears never to fire, and **the production code looks broken when it is
 correct.** Every guard test must assert the refusal left **no participant, no debit and no
 prize-pool change**, not merely the right message.
- **Defect 1's entry-fee ledger defect was proven, not inferred, and is now fixed** (1 Sep
 2026, `__tests__/services/entry-fee-ledger.test.ts`). Gate A wrote `referenceId`, which the
 `WalletTransaction` schema does not declare, and never set `competitionId`, which it
 does — so **every competition entry fee was unattributable to its competition.** Two
 things to state accurately: the harm was a **broken audit trail, not a wrong balance**
 (nothing computes money from that field — checked), and because *both* copies wrote
 `referenceId` identically this was **one bug duplicated, not mirror drift**, so the guard
 does not catch it and cannot. The unified service writes `competitionId`; historical rows
 stay unattributable, so **do not describe the audit trail as repaired retroactively.**
- **The same defect existed on the challenge side, wider, and was fixed 1 Sep 2026.**
 `challengeId` was declared on **neither** `WalletTransaction` copy while **nine** writers
 passed it — challenge entry (×2), the decline refund, and six finalization payouts across
 both apps — so **the entire challenge money trail was unattributable**, not just entry.
 Same harm statement as its sibling: audit trail, not balance; nothing queries
 `wallettransactions` by it; nothing to backfill, because the value never reached the
 database. Declared add-only in both copies with a matching index, proven by
 `__tests__/services/challenge-accept-guards.test.ts` and probed by un-declaring it again.
 **This is the third "one bug duplicated, not drift" case**, after `referenceId` and
 `failedReason`, so carry the general rule rather than the three instances: **the mirror
 guard proves the two apps agree with each other, never that either agrees with the code
 writing to them.** A green `check:mirrors` is not evidence that a field is stored.
- **Verify the throwaway sentence, not just the claim you set out to test.** That bug was
 found only because a passing remark inside a paragraph about something else — asserting
 that `challengeId` *was* declared, and that `competitionId` was an ObjectId, both false —
 was checked before being left on record. Documentation written during implementation is a
 defect-finding instrument; an unverified aside in it deserves the same suspicion as an
 unverified claim in code.
- **Defect 1 is built** (1 Sep 2026, awaiting owner test): `lib/services/contest-entry.service.ts`
 holds the transaction, with the result shapes, the pre-transaction guards and the
 post-commit work in `lib/services/contest-entry/` (`types.ts`, `guards.ts`,
 `side-effects.ts`) to keep every file under the 500-line limit. `enterCompetition` and
 `POST /api/competitions/[id]/join` are now thin wrappers over it, and the service re-exports
 the types so importers only ever need the one path.
 **The first thing to get right is that there were four
 writers, not the two the plan described** — the two known gates, a simulator batch route,
 and a dead admin copy. Each was resolved differently and the reasoning matters: the
 **dead admin copy was deleted** (nothing imported it, and it carried weaker guards that a
 future feature would have inherited); the **batch route was fixed in place** rather than
 routed through the service, because its bulk insert is the point of it. **Count the writers
 before unifying anything** — a plan that says "two paths" is an assumption until grep says so.
- **The batch route silently dropped six participant fields**, because it built seats with
 names the `CompetitionParticipant` schema does not declare (`pnlPercent` for
 `pnlPercentage`, `joinedAt` for `enteredAt`, and four more). Two rules: **extract an
 inline object builder into a named function before testing it** — the seat shape is now
 `lib/services/simulator/simulator-participant.ts` and a test compares its keys against
 `Model.schema.paths`, which no assertion on the saved document could do, since strict mode
 has already discarded the evidence by then. And **prove a field-name test fails** by
 injecting a bad name and watching it go red; a test that only ever passes proves nothing.
- **A duplicate entry is idempotent success, not an error** (owner decision, 1 Sep 2026):
 return the existing seat and take no second fee. That covers a double-click and a retried
 request. It needs handling in **two** places, and missing either leaves the hole open — the
 pre-flight participant check, *and* the **E11000 duplicate-key error** from the unique index
 when two requests pass the check in the same instant. Treat E11000 on that index as success.
- **`SuspicionScore` had two independent races, both now fixed** (1 Sep 2026,
 `__tests__/services/suspicion-score-race.test.ts`). It surfaced only once the entry path
 stopped serialising itself, which is the general lesson: **removing a bottleneck exposes
 every race the bottleneck was hiding** — the old path admitted 1 concurrent join in 20, so
 the losers never reached the fraud services at all. Five call sites, not the three first
 counted, each read-then-created its own document against a unique index; **17 of 20**
 concurrent detectors threw `E11000` and their contributions were discarded silently,
 because every caller is fire-and-forget. Fixed by making `getOrCreateScore` an atomic
 upsert and routing all five through it.
 **The second race is the more instructive one, and the upsert did not fix it.** `save()`
 writes only the paths one caller modified, so two detectors using *different* methods each
 persisted their own breakdown entry while `totalScore` — a whole-document field both
 computed from a stale copy — was last-write-wins. The document then **contradicted its own
 breakdown**: 40 and 25 stored correctly, total 40, so `riskLevel` sat at medium when 65
 crosses "high" and the account was never auto-restricted. Two rules follow: **a derived
 total is not safe just because its inputs are on separate paths**, and the fix is to
 recompute it **server-side from the persisted document** with a `$set` aggregation-pipeline
 update, which is order-independent and therefore converges no matter how the callers
 interleave. Two remaining read-then-create sites in `scripts/` are deliberately left —
 they are hand-run and single-threaded.
- **Sub-defect 1b is fixed** (1 Sep 2026): challenge accept now calls
 `checkAccountStanding` from `lib/services/contest-entry/guards.ts`, shared with the
 unified entry service so the two paid entry paths cannot drift apart again. Two things to
 keep right. It must pass **`enterChallenge`, never `enterCompetition`** — the flags differ
 on purpose, since `canEnterChallenges` blocks only on an explicit `false` so that
 restrictions predating the field stay allowed, and swapping them would quietly refuse every
 legacy-restricted account. And the guard runs **before any wallet read**, so a refusal
 cannot leave one of the two debits applied. Pinned by a test that asserts *which* action
 the route asks about, not merely that it refused — the refusal alone passes either way.
 Swept the sibling routes: challenge **create** already had both, **decline** and the admin
 challenge route only ever credit, so there is nothing else to fix.
- **Prerequisite B is fixed** (2 Sep 2026, awaiting owner test), and it exists because the
 owner reported a live incident rather than because anything in the plan predicted it: a
 player was locked out of paid entry by their **suspicion score alone**. The rule that comes
 out of it is short and load-bearing — **scores raise alerts, restrictions block.** The entry
 gate used to return `RISK_SCORE_BLOCKED` above `entryBlockThreshold`, which created no
 `UserRestriction`, so it appeared on no admin screen, notified nobody, could not be lifted,
 and **read `entryBlockThreshold` while never looking at `autoSuspendEnabled`** — automatic
 lockouts while the admin had automatic suspension switched off. The subtlest part is why it
 was unreportable: the dashboard's account-status card renders only while an alert is
 *pending or investigating*, so **dismissing the alert deleted the last explanation while
 the block stayed.** `entryBlockThreshold` is now a **Review** threshold (name kept in the
 schema, documented as historical — renaming it is a mirrored migration for a cosmetic gain),
 the dead `shouldBlockEntry` helper is deleted in both apps, and elevate-to-investigation now
 offers the full suspension options with the player notified either way. **Do not reintroduce
 a refusal inside the gate.** If automatic action is wanted, raise a restriction, so it stays
 reversible.
- **Five more live defects were found under Prerequisite B, and two generalise.** The
 first: `app/api/fraud/suspicion-score/route.ts` in the **player** app let any signed-in user
 read the high-risk list, **raise a rival's score to lock them out of a paid competition**,
 or clear their own — all three handlers commented "admin only", none checking anything but
 that a session existed. Deleted, since nothing called it and the admin app's copy is
 guarded. **That is the third time a comment has claimed an authorization check the code
 never performed**, after Prerequisite A and the internal-secret fallbacks, so carry it as a
 class: **a comment asserting authorization is not evidence one runs, and a route nothing
 calls is still reachable over HTTP.** The second generalisable one: auto-suspension set
 `suspensionEndsAt`, which `UserRestriction` does not declare, so strict mode discarded it
 and an unset `expiresAt` means **permanent** on this model — every auto-suspension was
 permanent while its reason text and history entry both promised seven days. **Same failure
 mode as mirror drift, from a different direction — one writer disagreeing with the schema
 rather than two copies disagreeing with each other — and `check:mirrors` cannot catch it.**
 The other three: **Auto-Suspend was near-unreachable at its default**, because it ran only
 on a risk-*band* change (bands 30/50/70) while `autoSuspendThreshold` defaults to 90, so a
 score entering critical at 72 was correctly refused for being under 90 and never examined
 again however high it climbed; **suspended and banned accounts could still accept paid 1v1
 challenges**, since `canEnterChallenges` was the only permission flag defaulting to
 *allowed* and 10 of 11 restriction writers never set it; and **`resetScore()` recorded every
 delta as `-0`**, computing `-this.totalScore` after zeroing the total.
- **Fix a wrong default at the schema, not across the call sites — but check for the one
 caller that meant it.** `canEnterChallenges` was wrong in 10 of 11 writers, so flipping the
 default fixed ten places with one line. The eleventh, the duplicate-KYC path driven by
 `duplicateKYCBlockChallenges`, sets the flag deliberately, and it is the reason the
 migration in `tools/fraud/fix-entry-blocked-users.ts --close-challenge-hole` filters on
 `reason: { $ne: "kyc_fraud" }`. Two rules follow. **A schema default fixes future rows
 only** — Mongoose already persisted the old value, so existing documents hold a real stored
 `true` indistinguishable from intent; the migration is not optional. And **a test that pins
 the new default must be accompanied by one pinning the explicit override**, or the two
 behaviours collapse into one assertion: probing this turned exactly 1 test red while the
 explicit-intent test stayed green, which is what proved they were independent.
- **A dead helper is an invitation, so delete it with the defect.** `shouldBlockEntry()`
 existed in both `fraud-settings.service.ts` copies, was called by nothing after the gate fix,
 and did exactly the thing that had just been removed. Leaving it would have made
 reintroducing the defect a one-line change that reads like using an existing API. Same
 reasoning as deleting the unprotected route rather than guarding it.

### Plug and play runs in both directions, and only one direction was designed

The owner's requirement, restated 2 September 2026 before X1: a new game must enter every
stat, ranking and calculation **with no additional coding**, and **the same must be true
when a game is removed.** The adding half was already designed. The removing half was
described nowhere — a search for it returned one unrelated hit about retiring a *metric*.
Design in `05` section 11, risk **R29**, invariants 8 and 9 in `11` section 5. Note it is
an **R-series** risk, not X-series — it applies whoever supplies the games, including
trading itself, so nothing about it depends on a third party. **Check the next free number
before adding a risk**: this one was written as X19, then R28, before landing on R29 —
R1–R28 were all taken, and `rg -o "R\d+"` over the register is the check.

- **Auto-inclusion and auto-exclusion are the same mechanism, and the second one corrupts
  earned data.** If cross-game totals are summed over *currently-enabled* games, then
  disabling a game **retroactively subtracts everything earned in it** — a player who
  reached level 12 partly through a provider game drops to level 9 because an operator
  switched it off. No error, no log, no notification. **Rate this High likelihood, not
  Medium:** summing the enabled set is the *natural* implementation, it reads correctly,
  it passes review, and it is only wrong on the day someone toggles a flag — months after
  the code ships and far from the author. The rule: **totals accumulate on settlement,
  never recompute on read**, and `getEnabledGameTypes()` gates creation, discovery and
  entry but **never a stats or leaderboard read path.** Disable affects discoverability
  and new entry, never history; a disabled game's rows are **retired, not deleted**, which
  is the second and stronger reason `gameKey` is immutable.
- **"No additional coding" is three different claims, and only one of them is free.** A
  new **title** from an existing provider is data-only — already an acceptance criterion in
  `12` s4. A new **provider** needs an adapter. A new in-house **game type** needs a
  module. `12` s4.2 already carries this as "the limit of no developer needed", so do not
  let a summary promise more than the architecture delivers.
- **Auto-inclusion is a property to preserve, not a feature to build.** There is exactly
  one way to lose it: **an aggregate that enumerates game types.** Any `switch` on game
  type, `if (gameType === "trading")`, or hard-coded game list is a place the next game
  silently fails to appear while the query runs and the page renders. Same failure shape as
  the trading-shaped services in `matchmaking.service.ts`.
- **Check the issued provider spec before assuming a restatement needs it changed.** The
  owner's description of the split — provider reports results, engine owns fees, winners,
  payouts, credits, stats and levelling — already matches section 1 of
  `ChartVolt-Game-API-Requirements.html` **verbatim**, which also already names
  one-against-one challenges and cross-game progression. **No re-issue, no version bump.**
  Verifying that cost one search and prevented a pointless version bump on a document
  providers may already be building against.

### The first X-phase code shipped 2 September 2026, and four things from it generalise

The admin navigation restructure (`12` s1/s1.1, built and recorded in **s1.1a**) separated
contest administration from trading and collapsed the six trading screens into one
collapsible **Trading** destination inside a **GAMES** group. It is a slice of **X6 taken
deliberately before X1 and before Stage 0 sign-off**, on the grounds that it is the only
part of X6 with no dependency on the foundation — it reorganises existing screens and adds
no field, model, service, route or server action — and that the admin app carries no player
traffic. **That reasoning does not extend to anything which writes data.**

- **A restatement can be wrong while its source is right, and that is the direction nobody
 checks.** `12` restated `New games plan/07`'s admin grouping and introduced two errors:
 an **Arsenal** tab that is not an admin section at all (`TradingArsenalContext.tsx` is
 chart tooling with no `menuGroups` entry and no `ADMIN_SECTIONS` id), and a **missing
 `market-data`**, which is real. `07` had both right. The paired-document table is written
 as though drift flows source → restatement; it flows both ways, so **verifying the source
 is not verifying the chapter you are building from.** Concretely: **a plan's list of
 screens is a hypothesis until checked against `menuGroups` and `ADMIN_SECTIONS`
 together** — presence in one but not the other is the interesting case, because the
 section can then either not be granted or not be reached.
- **Before building a mechanism a plan demands, check whether the codebase already has
 it.** Both of `12`'s hard cautions — tab state must be addressable, RBAC must stay
 per-section — were **already satisfied**. Sections are driven by `?activeTab=<sectionId>`,
 so reusing section ids as tab ids preserved every deep link with no new code; and
 `filteredMenuGroups` already reveals a parent only when `children.some(hasAccess)` and
 filters the children individually, which *is* per-tab gating, already proven by `settings`
 and `dev-zone-menu`. The `useState` trap the plan warned about is only reachable by adding
 a **second** state beside the one that exists. **The caution was right and the remedy was
 to reuse, not to build.**
- **A menu parent that renders nothing must not be a permission.** `trading-menu` is
 deliberately absent from `ADMIN_SECTIONS`. A grant that maps to no screen is exactly where
 privilege widening starts, and it reviews as harmless. Related and load-bearing:
 `ADMIN_SECTIONS` is a **Mongoose enum** on `allowedSections` and `customPermissions`, so
 it is **add-only** — removing a value orphans every employee document storing it. Note
 `admin-employee.model.ts` lives **only** in `apps/admin` and is *not* mirrored, so
 `check:mirrors` says nothing about it.
- **Classify a screen by the component it renders, never by the group it is filed under.**
 `analytics` sat in the "Trading" group and renders `CompetitionAnalytics` — contest
 analytics, which belongs to every game. When the group's name is the thing being
 corrected, that name is the least reliable evidence available. Same trap as the
 trading-shaped services in `matchmaking.service.ts`: **the label agrees with the old world
 and keeps agreeing after it ends.**

Pinned by `__tests__/admin/trading-section-nav.test.ts` (9 tests), **both halves probed by
reintroducing the defect** — restoring `trading-risk` under Settings turned exactly 1 red,
dropping a tab from the shared list turned 2 red. Verified by a full admin `next build` and
a `tsc --noEmit` matching the **225-error baseline exactly**, with none in the changed files
and, equally important, **none disappearing**. Still deferred: sidebar clicks do not write
the URL (pre-existing, affects all ~60 sections, belongs with X6.5), and the
`tradingEnabled` conditional that hides the destination waits on X1 — **"built" does not
include it.**

### Four things from X1 step 6 (4 September 2026) that generalise

The three guards - the game label on raw inserts (R7), the ESLint import boundary
(invariant 1), and the market-hours capability scope - are built, probed and closed.

- **"Count the writers" is now a rule with three instances, not an anecdote.** Defect 1's
 plan said two competition entry paths and there were **four**. Seam 3's plan said five
 finalization entry points and there were **ten**. R7 named **one** raw-driver insert and
 there were **six**. Every time, the plan's number was an assumption nobody had run `rg`
 against. **Before fixing the writer a document names, count them.** The two extra R7
 writers were admin **test harnesses**, which is the part that would have been shrugged
 off: the end-logic harness drives finalization, so seeding contests unlabelled meant it
 exercised the absent-label fallback rather than the path production takes - **a harness
 quietly testing something adjacent to the real thing.**
- **A risk entry can overstate its own severity, and an overstated risk is still a wrong
 fact.** `17` and `11` both claimed an unlabelled contest "gets settled by trading code and
 pays the wrong players". It does not: invariant 5 resolves an absent label to trading,
 which is correct for all six writers. R7 was never a live payout bug. The real harm is
 later and quieter - an aggregate that **groups by `gameKey`** silently drops the row, long
 after the commit, and **`gameKey` is immutable so it cannot be corrected in place.** Both
 documents and the internal HTML now say so. **Correcting a risk downward is as much a
 documentation duty as raising one**, because a risk register nobody believes is worse than
 a short one.
- **`no-restricted-imports` matches the import STRING, not the resolved path** - and write
 the probe before trusting the pattern. `**/lib/games/*` caught three violation forms and
 **missed `../games/trading`**, which has no `lib/` segment. A probe file with four
 violations and four legal imports showed 3 of 4 firing; reasoning about the glob would not
 have. Write the rule **blocked-by-default with the public surface negated**, never as a
 list of game folders: the list version leaves every future game unprotected until somebody
 remembers, and forgetting is silent.
- **Two more traps from adding invariant 2 the same day, and both produce a rule that looks
 right and does nothing.** First, **scope one level below the obvious one**:
 `lib/games/*/**` matches a module folder but not the layer's own public files, because
 `lib/games/index.ts` legitimately reads `WhiteLabel` for `getEnabledGameTypes()` - written
 as `lib/games/**` the rule bans that and reviews as correct. Second, **flat config is
 last-one-wins per rule**, so a block placed before an exemption that switches the same rule
 off is **silently dead**, and a dead config block still parses and still reads correctly.
 Pin the ordering with a test comparing the two blocks' indices. Related: ban **every**
 model rather than an allow-list of contest models - a module needing any document is
 already the design going wrong, and a list silently permits the next model added.
- **Scoping a gate to a property of the thing it guards can force the gate to MOVE.** The
 challenge-accept market check ran *before* the challenge was loaded, so it could not know
 the game - there is no way to make it conditional without first reading the document that
 carries the label. Two rules from placing it again: it must sit **before any wallet read**,
 the same ordering as `checkAccountStanding` in sub-defect 1b, so a refusal cannot leave one
 of two debits applied; and moving a guard **changes which error a bad request gets** - a
 missing challenge now returns 404 rather than a market-closed 400, which is an improvement
 but is a behaviour change and belongs in the work log either way.

**Two related rules for any capability gate.** It must **fail closed** on an unknown
value - wrongly applying a gate refuses something visibly and someone complains, wrongly
skipping it lets real money trade against a closed market. And it must **never take the
deciding value from caller input**: a client- or operator-supplied game type is a way to
skip the market gate on a trading contest by claiming to be a different game. Derive it from
the same helper that stamps the stored label, so the gate and the label cannot disagree.

**Owner decision, 4 September 2026: the weekend competition-create block is removed**, and
the reasoning generalises. **Capability-scoping is not always the right tool for a gate that
is wrongly applied.** The admin action refused an operator creating a *trading* competition
while the forex market was closed; scoping it to `needsMarketHours` would have fixed it for
provider games and **left it wrong for trading**. The question to ask first is not "which
games does this gate apply to" but **"is this gate about playing, or about scheduling?"** -
creating a contest is scheduling it, and order placement still refuses trades against a
closed market, so nothing was weakened. Do not reintroduce it. `assertForexMarketOpenForCreate()`
was deleted with it, on the `shouldBlockEntry` precedent. Related and worth noticing: **the
main app never had the check**, so the apps now agree rather than differing by accident - an
asymmetry a reviewer could otherwise "fix" in the wrong direction.

### Four things from X1 step 7, the backfill (4 September 2026)

`tools/games/backfill-game-labels.ts` covers backfills 1 and 3. **It has not been run
against production**; it is report-only until `--apply`.

- **The most important property of a backfill is what it refuses to touch.** `gameKey` is
 immutable because it is the join key for every historical stat, so a script that *can*
 rewrite it can destroy history silently. **"It only sets missing fields" is an assertion
 about a query filter**, and query filters are exactly the thing people get wrong. Pin it
 with a test that seeds a row already correctly labelled and asserts it survives untouched.
- **"Missing" has three shapes and only one is obvious**: absent, `null`, and `""`. A filter
 matching only `$exists: false` leaves the other two behind, **and those are the two that
 look correct in a document dump.** Probe each clause separately. Related: filter each
 field independently rather than as one `$or` over several - a half-labelled row is exactly
 what an interrupted earlier run leaves behind, and a combined filter either skips it or
 rewrites the field that was already right.
- **A schema default is not a stored value, and `.lean()` is where the difference shows.**
 Mongoose applies defaults when it *hydrates*, so an ordinary read of a pre-migration
 document returns the default with nothing stored - but `.lean()` skips hydration and
 returns the raw document, missing key and all. Much of this codebase reads with `.lean()`.
 **Never cite an ordinary read as evidence that a field is populated.**
- **Never let a migration carry its own copy of a constant.** Import it, and assert in a
 test that what the migration writes equals what the application writes. With its own
 literal, a later change to the app's default relabels history to a value nothing else
 uses - and **every row looks correctly labelled.**

**And a lesson about probing itself, which cost a false result here.** A multi-line
PowerShell replacement pattern with CRLF line endings did not match an LF file, so nothing
was modified and the test stayed green - reported as "the test is not doing its job" when
the test was fine. **A probe that fails to apply is indistinguishable from a test that does
not work.** Confirm the replacement actually changed the file before believing either
outcome; prefer single-line patterns.

**Deferring a planned migration is a documentation duty, not a silent omission.** Backfill 2
(`score` on participants) was **not** written, because trading ranks on its own metrics and
never reads `score`, and seam 2 - which would keep it current - is not built. Writing it
would produce a number nothing maintains and nothing reads, stale immediately and
authoritative-looking. Recorded in `18` section 1 with the reasoning, so the next reader does
not "fix" the gap.

**Say "code-complete", never "done", and say what it excludes.** X1 is code-complete; the
replay script has never been run and the backfill has not been applied. And the sentence that
must accompany any X1 completion claim: **finishing X1 does not mean external games work** -
a provider game does not plug in until **X4** and **X5**.

### Five things from X2, the provider abstraction (4 September 2026)

X2 is code-complete: `game_provider` and `provider_game` in both apps, three `WhiteLabel`
settings fields, and `lib/services/game-providers/` (contract, registry, mock adapter,
catalogue sync) mirrored into the admin app. **Nothing is player-visible** -
`externalGamesEnabled` defaults to false.

- **When a guard and a plan disagree about where code belongs, the guard usually wins.**
 `11` s3 put the provider code in a game module folder; invariant 2 bans model imports
 there, and the registry reads settings while the catalogue service writes `provider_game`.
 The rule is not the problem - the plan had not noticed the constraint. Result:
 **`lib/services/game-providers/` does I/O, `lib/games/provider/` stays pure.**
- **A blocked-by-default wildcard will eventually collide with a directory that merely
 shares a name.** `**/games/*` from invariant 1 began rejecting
 `@/database/models/games/provider-game.model` the moment X2 created that folder, and the
 rejection is indistinguishable from a real violation. Negate the specific collision
 (`!**/models/games/**`) rather than narrowing the rule, and expect this class of failure
 again whenever a new `games/` directory appears.
- **A glob written inside a JSDoc comment can truncate the file.** `lib/games/*/**` in prose
 contains `*/`, which closes the comment; the rest of the file then parses as garbage. The
 symptom is the trap: the typecheck **fell from 18 errors to 16**, reading as an
 improvement. **A file that fails to parse reports FEWER errors** - so treat an unexplained
 *drop* with the same suspicion as a rise, and note ESLint caught this where `tsc` did not.
- **`readonly field = SOME_CONST` infers the literal type and silently blocks subclassing.**
 It fails with TS2416 only when someone writes the second implementation - which for an
 adapter interface is the moment the whole abstraction is first tested. Annotate `: string`
 to match the interface.
- **Two switches per title, never one.** `providerStatus` is what the provider says;
 `chartvoltEnabled` is what we say, defaulting to false. One flag would let a third party
 put an untested game in front of paying players by editing their own database. The same
 shape as `enabled` on the provider and `externalGamesEnabled` globally: **a supplier's
 opinion is an input, never a decision.**

**Two rules for any catalogue or import sync**, both learned here and both about invisible
failure. Write a **named allow-list of the fields the remote system owns**, never a spread
of its payload - a spread overwrites operator edits on the next run and nobody notices until
someone's wording reverts. And **report missing rows, never delete them**: a row with
history cannot be removed without orphaning the stats joined to its key, and an item absent
from one response is as likely to be a partial failure upstream as a genuine withdrawal.

**Probing lesson, third instance, now with a fix.** Two X2 probes reported false passes: one
was aimed at the create path when the assertion covered the update path, and others failed
to match because a multi-line PowerShell pattern with CRLF met an LF file, or because an
emoji in the anchor did not survive the shell. **A probe aimed at the wrong code path is
indistinguishable from a test that does not work.** Use a helper that escapes the pattern
then relaxes every newline to `\r?\n`, keep anchors ASCII, and confirm the file actually
changed before believing either outcome.

### Six things from X3, rounds and result ingestion (4 September 2026)

X3 is code-complete: `game_round` and `provider_event` in both apps, `RoundService`, the
signed callback route, `ResultIngestionService` with chapter 06's eleven gates, and the
four-stage reconciliation net. **Rehearsals 1-6 of `07` s9 are green against the mock**, six
guards probed. Nothing player-visible.

- **"Enforced in the database" is a claim about a named index, and this one enforced
 something else.** `07` s4 credited `{ contestId, userId, attemptNumber }` with "one live
 round per player per contest". It does not: attempt 1 `launched` beside attempt 2
 `launched` satisfies that index perfectly, which is exactly the abandon-and-peek the rule
 exists to stop. Closed with a **partial unique index** on `{ contestId, userId }` filtered
 to the live statuses. Two traps inside the fix: scope it with `contestId: { $type:
 "objectId" }` or every practice round collides with the player's previous one, and it must
 be `$type` because **MongoDB forbids `$ne` in a `partialFilterExpression`** - the obvious
 spelling fails at index-build time with an error that never mentions the restriction.
 Same shape as the R7 correction: **a document asserting a protection is not evidence one
 exists.**
- **For a late result the dangerous window is `finalizing`, not `completed`** - and the
 obvious check misses it entirely. During `finalizing`, ranking is being computed from
 participant scores, so a score written then may or may not be counted **depending purely
 on timing**. That is worse than a genuinely late result, which is at least consistently
 excluded and alerted; this one is a coin flip that leaves no trace. Both contest models
 carry the state and both are treated as closed, along with `cancelled` and
 `emergency_ended`.
- **Two correct rules can force an awkward implementation, and the tidy-looking option is
 the wrong one.** An attempt must be consumed **on creation** (`03` s1.3) or a player
 abandons a bad round and retries free forever; a provider 5xx must consume **nothing**
 (`01` s6a). Since `{ contestId, userId, attemptNumber }` is unique, **a surviving row of
 any status permanently burns that attempt number** - so the rollback has to *delete* the
 pending round. Marking it `voided` reads like the careful choice and silently costs the
 player an attempt.
- **A backoff with no cap silently disables the stage it belongs to.** Poll backoff doubles
 from 30s; uncapped it eventually exceeds the grace window, so the round sits un-polled
 until the final sweep and stage 2 has effectively been switched off - no error, no log
 line. Related: **reaching the give-up stage is always critical**, even under `score_zero`
 where the contest settles on time, because it means the provider never reported and all
 three earlier stages failed. And the player is **always** told: a round silently scored
 zero is indistinguishable, from the player's seat, from being cheated.
- **Name an obligation you are deferring, in a field, so the next phase cannot miss it.**
 The `exclude` unresolved policy owes a refund, but paying it in the reconciliation service
 would put a second money writer beside settlement - and removing a player changes the
 prize pool, so the refund and the re-split are one transaction. It returns
 `refundOwed: true` instead. Same reasoning kept contest round fields
 (`attemptsPolicy`, `unresolvedRoundPolicy`, `playWindowEnd`) as a passed-in config rather
 than six new mirrored fields for a path nothing calls yet. **Do not let a summary imply
 `exclude` is fully implemented.**
- **The `**/games/*` ESLint wildcard collided for the second time**, now with
 `lib/services/games` after `database/models/games` in X2. Negated again - and the wildcard
 is **kept deliberately** now the cost is known. Anchoring it to `**/lib/games/*` would end
 the collisions, but the rule matches the **import string**, so a nested file writing
 `../../games/trading` would stop being caught. **That trade is the wrong way round for a
 guard:** a false positive is noisy and fixed in a minute, a missed violation is silent and
 is the whole point of the rule. Expect one negation per new `games/` directory.

**And the fixture lesson, recurring for the third time: a fixture trimmed to the fields the
test reads will fail everything at once for one unrelated reason.** The `Competition` fixture
omitted `slug`, `startTime`, `endTime` and `startingCapital` - none of which a round
touches - and **34 tests failed on a single validation error**. Mongoose validates the
document, not the subset you care about. It keeps recurring because trimming a fixture always
feels like tidying.

### Five things from the X6 provider-admin slice (4 September 2026)

Provider registration, credentials and the per-title enable switch are code-complete
(`12` s4.1a). Two of `12` s4's five destinations; health, round inspector and manual
resolution deliberately left. (**The inspector and manual resolution were built the next
day** — `12` s4.2a. Health is still outstanding.)

- **A structural test that greps source code must strip comments first, and the failure
 teaches the wrong lesson if you skip it.** The assertion "no route mentions
 `requireAdminAuth`" went red on a *comment* in the route explaining why `requireAdminAuth`
 is the wrong helper. **A test that reads prose fails in both directions**: it flags a
 correct file that discusses the anti-pattern, and it passes a broken one whose only mention
 of the right helper is in a comment. Strip block and line comments, then match. This is the
 same class as the aside-verification rule - documentation written beside code is an
 instrument, and here it was being read *as* the code.
- **`requireAdminAuth` is not an authorization check, and it is the natural thing to reach
 for.** It asks only whether the caller is an admin at all, so an employee granted one
 unrelated section passes it; `requireSectionAccess(id)` is the grant. **Fourth instance of
 the class** after Prerequisite A, the internal-secret fallbacks and the unprotected
 suspicion-score route, so carry the rule not the instances. And pin it with **two** tests:
 one for the helper, one that **counts exported handlers and counts guards**, because a file
 whose `GET` is guarded and whose `PATCH` is not passes the first check while leaving the
 mutation wide open.
- **When a UI can never display a stored secret, a blank field must mean "keep".** The
 alternative is silent: an operator editing only the environment submits every secret box
 empty, and if empty meant "clear" that harmless edit breaks every inbound callback with no
 error raised anywhere. Offer removal explicitly instead. The general form: **the safe
 reading of an empty input depends on whether the user can see what is already there**, and
 write-only fields invert the usual answer.
- **A switch that cannot work must refuse, with the reason, rather than merely being
 disabled.** Enabling a provider with no installed adapter would leave
 `resolveEnabledProvider` refusing every round with a message no operator can act on - a
 control that appears to work and silently does nothing, the same shape as the
 trading-shaped services in `matchmaking.service.ts`. Same for a missing callback secret,
 where every result then fails signature verification and **looks identical to an attack in
 the logs.** Greying out the control is not enough; the card has to say which of the two is
 missing.
- **Do not offer delete on anything joined to history.** A provider that has run a contest is
 reached by `providerKey`, and `gameKey` is immutable, so deleting the row orphans the stats
 while every screen still renders a key it cannot resolve. Disable is the reversible
 operation. Same reasoning as the catalogue sync reporting missing titles rather than
 removing them, and as retiring a disabled game's rows rather than deleting them (R29).

**Two more, both about how a defect was found rather than what it was.** The live bug in this
slice - the first-ever credential save being recorded as a rotation - was caught only because
the test asserted the **whole** credential object rather than the three fields it cared
about; the extra `rotatedAt` was the evidence. **Assert the whole object when the object is
small enough**, because a field you did not ask about is the only way to notice one you did
not expect. And **probe with a helper that relaxes newlines and confirms the file changed**:
setting `$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'` in a probe script made `npx`'s harmless
`ExperimentalWarning` on stderr abort the run, which printed nothing and looked exactly like
six passing probes.

**One more ESLint wildcard collision, the third, exactly as predicted.** `**/games/*` began
rejecting `@/components/admin/games/GameProvidersSection` the moment that folder existed,
after `database/models/games` in X2 and `lib/services/games` in X3. Negated again; the
wildcard stays, for the reason already recorded. **Three collisions is now enough to state
the expectation plainly: any new directory named `games/` anywhere in the tree will trip
this rule on its first import, and the fix is one negation, not a narrower rule.**

### Five things from the X6 contest wizard (4 September 2026)

The provider contest wizard, its `configSchema`-generated settings form and the `03` s4.1
pre-flight checklist are code-complete, 49 tests, all 15 guards probed. It creates a
**draft**; nothing publishes or settles it.

- **A hand-written input interface is where an invented field survives a typecheck.** The
 pre-flight checklist takes its facts as arguments rather than importing the model - which
 is right, because it keeps the checks pure and cheap to test - but it also means the
 compiler has nothing to disagree with. The first draft read `title.billsPerRound`, **a
 field `provider_game` does not have**, and that warning would have been permanently
 unreachable. **A checklist item gated on a field nobody populates is an item that never
 runs.** Checking the model's real field list also turned up `lastSuccessfulRoundAt`, which
 *does* exist and is what the sandbox-freshness check now reads. Same class as the R7
 correction and the `challengeId` aside: **an unverified field name in a plan or an
 interface is a claim, not a fact.**
- **Narrowing a `required` field is a change to the OTHER game's contract.** Making
 `startingCapital` conditional reads as "provider games don't need it". What it does is
 move a guarantee trading relies on out of the schema and into a predicate - and the
 `?? "trading"` in it is load-bearing, not defensive, because **invariant 5 resolves an
 absent label to trading.** Written `this.gameType === "trading"`, an unlabelled trading
 contest saves with no capital and every downstream calculation divides by it. Pin it in
 **both** copies: a conditional requirement that differs between the apps is a validation
 rule whose outcome depends on which process saved the document, and `check:mirrors`
 compares field paths and enum values - **not predicate bodies.**
- **A "no additional coding" claim has exactly one failure mode: something that enumerates
 games.** For a schema-driven form that means a `switch` on game code. The renderer branches
 on the declared field *type* and a test asserts the file contains no `gameCode`, `gameKey`
 or `providerKey` at all. Without it the first title needing a special case makes the
 acceptance criterion quietly false while every existing test still passes. Same shape as
 invariant 9 and the trading-shaped services in `matchmaking.service.ts`.
- **A schema parser must fail closed, and permissiveness here is worse than refusing.**
 Unsupported keywords (`allOf`, `pattern`, `oneOf`) refuse the whole schema rather than
 being skipped. Skipping renders a form missing half the real constraints and then validates
 against the half it understood - **reporting success while accepting settings the provider
 rejects at play time.** The same instinct that makes the market-hours gate fail closed on
 an unknown game. Related, from the checklist: **keep warnings and refusals separate**, or
 the whole thing gets bypassed. Three items are advisory - the platform master switch being
 off (scheduling ahead of a launch is legitimate), a stale sandbox round, and the per-round
 cost acknowledgement - and turning any into a refusal pushes an operator to switch external
 games on platform-wide just to draft a contest. Conversely the hard refusals **accumulate
 rather than stopping at the first**: an operator fixing one per submission gives up.
- **Refuse a contest missing its round settings; never default them.** Falling back to
 single-attempt with a grace period is the tidy-looking option and it is wrong - the contest
 runs, players play, and the settings governing their money are ones no operator chose. This
 is what closes X3's `RoundContestConfig` deferral, and the general rule is the same one
 behind the `entryBlockThreshold` fix: **a stored value and an absent one are different
 facts.**

**A deviation worth the precedent, because the instinct is to absorb it.** `12` s2 describes
**one** wizard whose fourth step becomes dynamic; **two** were built, with a game picker
routing to either. That section sets two acceptance criteria - *no trading field in a
provider contest* and *trading creation unchanged* - which one wizard satisfies only after
refactoring the 2,892-line form live trading contests depend on. Two paths satisfy both
immediately, and merging later becomes a UI refactor against a green suite rather than the
first thing attempted. **Recorded in `12` s2.1 rather than quietly rewriting the plan to
match** - and the picker **redirects straight to trading when no provider game exists**,
because a "choose your game" screen with one choice is friction on the path operators use
daily, and friction there is how a new screen gets worked around.

**Probing lesson, fourth instance, and this time the harness lied twice.** One probe
reported `PROBE DID NOT APPLY` (4-space indent in the pattern, 2 in the file) and **every**
probe reported "OTHER test", because `Out-String` wraps at the console width and a long test
name arrived split across two lines, so the literal match silently missed it. Both outcomes
look exactly like a broken test. Fixes are in `tools/probe-contest-wizard.ps1`: **collapse
all whitespace in captured output before matching**, and **assert the file actually changed
before believing any outcome**. **A probe harness needs its own probe.**

### Five things from X5's first half - publish, entry and ranking (4 September 2026)

A provider contest can now be published, entered and ranked. **Nobody can play one and
nobody is paid**; settlement and the `exclude` refund remain.

- **"Additive only" does not make a model general - it only stops it breaking.** X1 added
  `score` and `gameKey` to `CompetitionParticipant` and removed nothing, which was right.
  But three trading-capital fields were still `required: true` **with no default**, so a
  provider participant **could not be saved at all**. The generalisation was purely
  additive and the model stayed trading-only. **When you add a field for a new case, check
  what the old fields still DEMAND** - the interesting requirement is the one nobody
  touched. Same shape as the R7 correction: the absent thing, not the changed thing.
- **A schema default fires before validation, so the obvious test of a `|| "trading"`
  fallback proves nothing.** Deleting `gameKey` and expecting the predicate to see
  `undefined` fails: Mongoose fills the default first. The test passed with and without the
  `||`. An **empty string** does reach the predicate, because defaults fill `undefined`
  only - and "" is a genuine missing-value shape, the same one the label backfill exists
  for. **When a probe stays green, decide whether the test is weak or the CLAIM is wrong
  before assuming the first** - here it was the test; one probe later it was the claim.
- **A guard that changes no answer should say so rather than claim a fix.** The `isAtRisk`
  NaN guard could not be probed red, because `NaN < 60` is false exactly as the guard's
  explicit `false` is. It was kept - the accident holds only for `<`, and an "is healthy"
  check written with `>` would silently be wrong - but the comment now records that it is
  clarity, not a bug fix. **An overstated comment is a wrong fact**, the same duty as
  correcting R7 downward.
- **One module per GAME CATEGORY, never one per title or per provider - and the thing that
  varies must travel on the data.** Direction is a property of the title (a puzzle scores
  upward, a time trial downward), so a module-level constant would force one module per
  title. It rides on `RankableParticipant.scoreDirection` instead, threaded in at
  finalization. Two rules fall out: **store the RAW score** (a persisted `-92.4` shows a
  race time as negative on every screen and poisons cross-game totals), and **negate only
  at comparison**, so the engine keeps performing exactly one descending sort for every
  game and no game reaches in to change how sorting works. The registry tripwire now
  asserts `["trading", "provider"]` and says that a third entry means the engine has
  learned something game-specific.
- **A setting that a game cannot honour must not be offered.** The provider module ignores
  `rankingMethod` completely, pinned by a test asserting all six methods return the same
  value. Trading's six methods are six questions you can ask of a trading account; a
  provider game reports one number. Six labels for one behaviour is a control that appears
  to work and does nothing - the same failure as enabling a provider with no adapter, and
  as the trading-shaped services in `matchmaking.service.ts`.

**And two rules for anything that publishes.** Re-run the checklist against the **stored**
record, not the caller's input and not the validation done on creation day - a draft can
outlive the switches that made it valid, and reading the saved document also asks the
question creation could not, which is whether the settings actually persisted. And
**there is deliberately no unpublish**: a visible contest can be paid into, so hiding it
strands paid entrants. Cancel-with-refund is the reversible operation, the same reasoning
that gives providers a disable switch and no delete.

**One more invented field caught by the schema, the fifth instance.** `publishedAt` was
written into the publish update and removed: `Competition` does not declare it, so strict
mode would have discarded it while the function reported success. It was dropped rather
than declared, because `updatedAt` already carries the timestamp and the audit log already
carries who did it - **add a mirrored field only when something will read it.**

### Six things from X5's second half - round launch and settlement (4 September 2026)

A provider contest can now be **published, entered, played and paid**. Only the `exclude`
refund remains. The settlement stages live in `lib/services/settlement/`, mirrored, and
`competition-end.actions.ts` went from **1,885 to 1,174 lines**.

- **Extraction versus reimplementation is a claim about what your GREEN TESTS MEAN, not
  about tidiness.** The five trading payout tests and the golden ranking regression staying
  green is the only evidence that no payout moved - and it is only evidence if nothing else
  changed in the same commit. That is why a **known one-character defect was preserved
  verbatim**: the Game Master fee reads `limits.referralFeePercentage || 5`, so a package
  configured at 0% falls through to 5%. Fixing it there would have bought one line and cost
  the proof for 900. **Carry the general form: while extracting, a behaviour change is not a
  small addition, it is the destruction of the only guarantee the extraction offers.**
- **Generalising code is a better bug-finding instrument than looking for bugs.** Four
  defects surfaced and **three were pre-existing TRADING defects**: `finalLeaderboard`
  declared only trading's numeric fields, so `isTied`, `qualificationStatus` and
  `disqualificationReason` were computed and **silently discarded on every finalization ever
  run**; `split_weighted` tie distribution divided by tied participants' summed capital with
  no guard, so a tie between wiped-out accounts paid `NaN`; and the early-end worker logged
  `finalizeResult?.message` where failures carry `error`, recording every failure as the
  bare string "Failed to finalize". None was reachable by reading the trading path alone.
- **When a probe stays green, ask whether the TEST is weak before assuming the claim is
  wrong - the answer differs, and it differed twice on one day.** In X5's first half the
  claim was wrong (the `isAtRisk` guard changes no answer). Here the test was weak: deleting
  the pre-lock game gate left the suite green, because the post-lock gate refuses too and
  the contest lands back on `active` either way. The gates are **not** redundant - only the
  pre-lock one never *claims* the contest, so a crash between claim and release cannot
  strand it in `finalizing` - but that is a difference in **writes, not in state.** The fix
  was to assert `updatedAt` never moved. **A guard whose whole value is "no write happened"
  needs a test that can observe a write.**
- **A refusal code the UI must branch on is not an implementation detail to collapse.** The
  first `LaunchRefusal` folded "attempts exhausted", "a round is already live" and "the
  provider is down" into `contest_not_open`, which **lied** - the contest is open, the
  player simply cannot start another round. The three need three different affordances
  (disable, offer to resume, offer to retry). Pass the lifecycle refusals through unchanged;
  a generic code forces the UI to guess and it will guess wrong.
- **Two disagreeing copies of one rule prove one of them is a bug, and that is the cheapest
  proof available.** The GM `||` looked like a possible judgement call until
  `challenge-finalize.actions.ts:994` and `:1000` turned up doing the same lookup with `??`.
  One stored configuration, two answers. **When you find a suspicious operator in duplicated
  logic, read the sibling copy before deciding whether it is deliberate** - and note this is
  the fourth "one rule, two copies" finding, after `referenceId`, `failedReason` and
  `challengeId`, none of which `check:mirrors` can see, because it compares models.
- **Chase a typecheck count that FALLS, with the same suspicion as one that rises - but
  expect the boring explanation too.** Admin went 225 -> 223. Six errors had merely **moved
  line numbers** because the file shrank by 711 lines, which a count cannot show; only two
  genuinely vanished, and those marked the worker logging bug above - real code reaching for
  a field its own type denied it. **Diff the error lists, never the counts**, and the
  reliable way to get the baseline is `git stash push --include-untracked`, because untracked
  new files stay in the program and report their own errors otherwise.

**And the accuracy rule that nearly got broken writing this up.** "A provider contest can be
published and paid" was about to be recorded without checking that an operator can *do* it.
Nothing under `apps/admin/components/` fetched the publish route, and no player screen calls
the launch route - the whole lifecycle was reachable **by API and by test, not by clicking.**
**Before writing that a capability exists, grep for its caller**, or a demo discovers the gap
instead. (**Both halves were closed 5 Sep 2026** - the publish button, then the player play
screen - so the whole lifecycle is now clickable. Correct as history, stale as a present fact.)
Same class as the paired-document drift rule: the restatement was going to be wrong
while every underlying fact was right.

### Five things from X5's third piece - the unresolved-round policies (4 September 2026)

X5 is code-complete. All three of chapter `07`'s answers to a round that never reports are
honoured: `lib/services/settlement/unresolved-rounds.ts` reads the state,
`exclusion-refund.ts` moves the money, both mirrored.

- **An obligation recorded in four places is no more likely to be complete than one
 recorded nowhere.** Every document tracked the `exclude` refund as X5's last item.
 Nothing consumed `blocksSettlement` either, so a `hold_and_alert` contest **settled on
 time and paid out** while the policy promised it would be held - invisible purely because
 it had never been written down. This is the counting rule in a new shape: after "count the
 writers" (four entry paths, ten finalize sites, six raw inserts), now **count the
 siblings of the obligation you are closing.** A to-do list is evidence of what somebody
 wrote down, never of what is outstanding.
- **A cross-process seam can only ever be a persisted fact.** `reconcileRound` returns
 `refundOwed` and `blocksSettlement`, and four documents implied settlement would consume
 them - but they are return values in a worker that has exited long before a contest
 settles, and nothing persists them. Settlement re-derives both from
 `round.status = "unresolved"`, the one thing stage 4 writes. The bonus, and the reason to
 prefer this even where a parameter would work: a contest finalized by a path the net never
 drove - the lazy auto-finalize, a manual admin trigger - honours the policy without
 knowing it exists. **A flag passed in makes every other caller silently skip the rule.**
- **A `catch` block is not a refusal handler, and this file's own warning applied to
 itself.** `provider-finalize.ts` was written with the lock release called out as "easy to
 leave out and impossible to notice in a test that only checks the happy path" - and it
 then committed a `success: false` return and left the contest at `finalizing` for ever,
 unclaimable by any caller, cron or human, with nobody paid. Latent only because nothing
 returned a refusal yet. **Any function that both holds a lock and calls something
 returning a result object needs the release on the returned-refusal path, not just the
 thrown one.**
- **A status field that reads as authoritative removes nobody.** `exclude` did not merely
 fail to refund: `calculateRankings` reads participant `status` only for the liquidation
 rule, so a player marked `refunded` stayed ranked and could be **paid a prize as well as
 being owed their fee back**. Exclusion has to be a filter on the list handed to ranking.
 Before trusting a status to gate anything, **grep for who reads it** - the same lesson as
 grepping for a capability's caller before writing that it exists.
- **When a probe stays green, the three causes need three different responses, and only
 running it separates them.** All three appeared on one day. *Weak test:* deleting the pool
 reduction passed, because the integrity cap recomputes the same figure from the
 already-reduced participant count - fixed by a new test seeding a pool **below** the fees
 collected, where the cap has headroom and cannot cover for it. *Unreachable test:* the
 lock release and the in-transaction hold gate are caught by the pre-lock gate in every
 ordinary path, so they needed a mocked race answering differently with and without a
 session. *Wrong claim:* `contestId` as a plain string passed because **Mongoose casts it
 when the query executes** - the comment asserting a silent miss was false and was
 corrected. Note the near-miss inside that one: the raw MongoDB driver does **not** cast,
 so the test helper genuinely needs a real ObjectId, and conflating the two halves is what
 produced the wrong comment in the first place.

**And a documentation duty that comes with shipping a deferral: an operator-facing warning
that has become false is worse than no warning.** The pre-flight checklist told operators
the `exclude` refund was "not yet automatic". Left alone, an operator either avoids a policy
that now works or pays a second refund by hand on top of the automatic one. **The test
pinning it was flipped, not deleted** - same rule as every defect test, since the comment
explaining why the caution existed is the most valuable part. The warning itself was kept
and rewritten, because removing a paid entrant and shrinking the pot is consequential
whether or not it is automated.

### Five things from R31, the configured 0% referral rate (5 September 2026)

R31 is closed: a Game Master rate configured at 0% now means 0% wherever it is stored, read
or shown. `lib/services/gamemaster/subscription-limits.ts` (mirrored) is the single writer of
a subscription's cached limits, `calculate.ts`'s three fallbacks became one helper, 14 tests,
8 probes, and `tools/gamemaster/report-stale-subscription-limits.ts` is report-only.

- **A risk register entry is a claim about the code, not a fact about it — and this one named
 the wrong branch.** R31 said a 0% *package* was paid 5%. It was not: the current-package
 read already tested `!== undefined`, so 0 returned 0. The `||` sites were the **fallbacks
 onto the cached `subscription.limits`**, reachable only when the package has been deleted or
 the subscription has no `packageId`. **A fix aimed at the register's sentence would have
 changed the one branch that was already right.** Second instance after R7, so carry the
 class rather than the two cases: **check a risk against the code before fixing it, and
 correcting it downward is the same documentation duty as raising one.** The register, the
 chapter, `11`, PROGRESS and the internal HTML were all corrected together.
- **`||` was wrong about 0 and accidentally right about `NaN`, so the one-character fix is
 wrong.** These values arrive from `parseFloat` on an admin form, so `NaN` is one keystroke
 away, and `??` passes it straight onto a required `Number` path — where every multiplication
 downstream silently becomes `NaN` and nothing checks. The general form: **when replacing a
 truthy check, enumerate everything it was catching**, because a falsy guard usually catches
 more than the one value that motivated the change. `Number.isFinite`, not `??`.
- **The tracked half was the read; the untracked half was the write, and the write was
 worse.** Six routes copied a package's config onto a subscription with
 `config.referralFeePercentage || 5`, so **buying a 0% package stored 5%** — durable, and a
 stored 5 is indistinguishable from a deliberate 5. It also reached the **challenge** path
 *through the data*: that path resolves with `??` and was correct, and faithfully paid the
 wrong number it had been given. **When two code paths disagree, check whether one of them is
 merely reading what the other wrote** before concluding which is buggy. And **count the
 writers**, again: one file named, seven found.
- **A control that advertises a value and then refuses it is why nobody had hit the bug.**
 The admin editor declared the fee input `min={0}`, rendered a stored 0 as **5**, and wrote
 `parseFloat(e.target.value) || 5`, so **typing 0 was rewritten to 5 on the keystroke** — a
 0% package could only be created by calling the API directly. That is what makes R31
 **latent rather than an active loss**, and saying which is the difference between a register
 entry that is believed and one that is not. Same shape as enabling a provider with no
 adapter, and as a `rankingMethod` a provider game ignores.
- **Two sources must disagree before a test can prove which one was read.** A probe removing
 the `!== undefined` check stayed green because the test seeded the package at 0% *and* the
 cache at 0%: the wrong branch produced the right answer. Seeding the cache at 5 turned it
 red. This is the precedence-test form of the same trap that made the pre-lock hold gate
 unprovable by end status alone — **when a probe stays green, ask whether the fixture can
 distinguish the branches at all**, before concluding the test is weak or the claim is wrong.

**And a verification note that saved a false finding twice in one pass.** The admin typecheck
and the lint output both appeared to gain entries. Every one was a **line shift** — three
`fix-purchases` errors moved by one line because of an added import, and a pre-existing
unescaped apostrophe moved 12 lines because of an added comment. **Diff the lists and compare
the message text, not the counts and not the line numbers**, or a comment looks like a
regression.

### Five things from the X6 publish control (5 September 2026)

An operator can now publish a draft provider contest by clicking. `PublishContestButton.tsx`,
`lib/admin/contest-game-label.ts`, a game-aware `CompetitionsListSection`, 21 tests, 21
probes all red. **No player screen starts a round**, so the play step is still API-only.

- **A trading-shaped SCREEN fails exactly like a trading-shaped service, and this one had
  been failing for a day before anybody looked.** `GET /api/competitions` applies no filter,
  so provider drafts were already rendering in the admin list - and the component's
  `Competition` interface did not admit `"draft"`, so it fell through `getStatusColor`'s
  default into **the same grey the screen uses for a completed contest.** An unpublished
  contest looked finished. No error, no log line. The rule: **when a type denies a state the
  screen is already being handed, the screen is not type-safe, it is uninformed** - and the
  `default:` arm of a status switch is where the new state goes to die quietly.
- **A helper's NAME is part of its contract, and reusing an existing one is the natural
  mistake.** `isProviderContest` already existed and answers a **stricter** question - label
  *and* provider key *and* game code, because a labelled contest with no keys cannot launch a
  round. A screen asks *what kind of row is this*, and a half-built provider contest is still
  one for badging and for keeping out of the trading editor. Importing the strict helper would
  have compiled, reviewed correctly, and rendered a keyless provider contest as a **trading**
  one, Edit button and all. **When two questions differ, give them two names** - and the
  general form: before writing `=== "provider"` inline, check which of the two questions you
  are actually asking.
- **A control that would corrupt data must be withheld with its reason stated, not greyed
  out.** `/competitions/edit/[id]` renders the trading editor and `PUT
  /api/competitions/[id]` does a blind `Object.assign` of that form's body, so the Edit link
  is a **corruption path** on a provider contest rather than a confusing screen. Third
  instance of this shape after a provider with no adapter and a `rankingMethod` a provider
  game ignores, so carry it as a class: **a disabled control teaches nothing; a refusal that
  names the missing thing is the feature.**
- **A summary that counts by status is an aggregate that enumerates its cases.** The list's
  four cards counted active, upcoming and completed, so a draft landed in `Total` and in none
  of the others - the same failure as an aggregate that enumerates game types, one field
  along. **Adding a status means auditing every place that switches on status**, and the
  count cards are the one nobody thinks of.
- **A probe that stays green has a THIRD cause, and it appeared here: the test does not
  exist.** After "weak test" and "wrong claim", this one was **a missing test** - blanking the
  game badge's condition left the suite green because the probe had been aimed at a test
  asserting the strict helper is not *imported*, a different claim which the other two call
  sites keep satisfying. **A probe aimed at the wrong test is indistinguishable from a test
  that does not work**, so name the expected failing test in the probe and check that the red
  one is the one you meant.

**And two rules for structural tests specifically, both learned by getting them wrong here.**
**Strip comments before matching** - these files explain the anti-patterns in prose, so a test
that reads prose flags a correct file for discussing the mistake and passes a broken one whose
only mention of the right thing is in a comment. And **never scan a fixed number of characters
to find a guard**: the first Edit-guard test looked 300 characters back, began mid-identifier,
and reported a guard missing that was present. Assert **position within the construct**
instead - condition, then branch separator, then the guarded thing - and count the occurrences
so a second unguarded one cannot hide behind the first.

### Five things from R32 and R33, the missing score seam (5 September 2026)

Both closed. `lib/services/games/participant-score.service.ts` carries a round's score up to
`participant.score` from **gate 11b** of the single ingestion function, and
`resolveContestScoreDirection` reads the ranking direction from the catalogue title once per
contest. Found while mapping the code for the round inspector, **a day after X5 was called
code-complete**, and both would have paid the wrong players.

- **"Code-complete" is not "correct", and the gap between them is exactly where a green suite
  is most dangerous.** X5 shipped with no code path writing `participant.score` at all, so
  every provider participant would have settled on zero, tied at rank 1, and split the pool
  equally however well they played - and with `scoreDirection` read from a field neither
  participant copy declares, so every lower-is-better game paid the slowest player first.
  Neither raises an error. **Say what a completion claim excludes**, and never let it imply
  the path has been exercised end to end.
- **A fixture that supplies the value under test has tested the consumer, not the producer.**
  The settlement suites seed `score: 900 / 500 / 100` and rank them, which proves ranking works
  *given* scores and is structurally silent on whether one ever arrives. Second instance after
  trading finalization's `pnl`, so carry the rule: **when a value crosses a seam, one test must
  start on the far side of it.**
- **A raw-driver fixture can prove anything, because it is not bound by the schema the
  application writes through.** `db.collection(...).insertMany` bypasses Mongoose strict mode
  entirely, so the settlement test stored `scoreDirection` on participants - a field no
  production path could ever write - and then read it back green. Two corollaries: seed through
  the model or seed the thing production actually reads, and **check the collection name**,
  because these schemas set explicit ones (`provider_game`, `game_round`) and writing to a
  guessed pluralised collection has the same symptom as writing the wrong value.
- **An explicitly-typed `.lean<{...}>()` is a place a field that does not exist looks real.**
  The compiler checks the hand-written generic, not the schema, which is why neither typecheck
  ever objected to the direction read and the usual "errors that *disappear* after a model
  sync" signal never appeared. **Do not treat a clean typecheck as evidence that a read matches
  its schema.**
- **When the code and a chapter disagree, check which one is wrong before making them agree -
  the drift runs both ways.** The first fix declared `scoreDirection` on both participant
  copies, which is the smaller diff and satisfies the read that already existed. `05` s2 rules
  it out, and the failure it prevents is worse than the one it costs: per-row storage lets two
  rows in **one** leaderboard disagree, so half the board negates and half does not. A
  uniformly wrong direction is coherent and visibly wrong; an incoherent one looks plausible
  and cannot be explained to a player. **Here the chapter was right and the code was wrong**,
  the opposite of the paired-document drift the table below is written for.

**And the general form of why both defects were invisible, which is now the fourth instance:
an aside in a comment is a claim, not a fact.** `provider-settlement.service.ts` opened by
asserting that ingestion already wrote `participant.score`. After `challengeId`, the R7
severity and `billsPerRound`, carry the class rather than the cases - and **leave the
correction visible in the file** rather than quietly fixing the tense, because the next reader
needs to know the sentence was once believed.

### Five things from the X6 round inspector and manual resolution (5 September 2026)

An operator can now list the rounds needing a decision, read the raw provider deliveries and
**end** one with a mandatory reason. `12` s4.2a, 21 tests, 12 probes red. **It cannot enter a
score, deliberately**, and that scoping is the load-bearing part: `applyResult` is the single
ingestion door and it lives in the **main app only**, so a score box in admin would build the
second door `02` s10 rule 3 forbids, in the app with the widest privileges and the least
traffic. Ending a round is sufficient because `assessUnresolvedRounds` derives both its answers
from `round.status === "unresolved"`.

- **A probe harness can destroy the file it is probing and report success — and the tell is the
  failure COUNT, not the failure.** Next.js dynamic routes contain `[roundId]`, which
  **PowerShell parses as a wildcard character class**, so `Get-Content $File` matched nothing
  and returned `$null` while `Set-Content -LiteralPath` wrote it back happily. The route was
  emptied, then "restored" to nothing. Every probe went red **on the expected test**, for
  entirely the wrong reason. **5–7 tests red for a one-line change is the signal**; the honest
  number is 1 or 2. Two rules: `-LiteralPath` on the **read** as well as the write, and refuse
  to write when the read came back empty. Fifth probing instance, so carry the general form: **a
  probe that reports more damage than it caused is not reporting on your guard.**
- **An import is not a use, and it defeated three assertions in one file.**
  `toContain("canTransitionRound")` stayed true when the call was replaced by a hand-rolled
  status check, because the name is still in the import line; `toContain("MIN_REASON_LENGTH")`
  stayed true when the check became `if (false)`, because the constant is still named in the
  error *message*; and `indexOf("resolveRoundManually")` found the **import on line 8** and
  ordered against that. **Match the call with its arguments, and assert the operator, not the
  operand** — `/reason\.length\s*<\s*MIN_REASON_LENGTH/`, not the constant's name.
- **A shared list beats a duplicated one even when the duplication has a good excuse.** The
  action ids and their operator-facing consequences were first written twice, because the
  service imports Mongoose models and a client component must not pull those into the browser.
  Real constraint, wrong answer — this is the **"one rule, two copies"** shape behind four
  defects here already (`referenceId`, `failedReason`, `challengeId`, the GM `||`), none of
  which `check:mirrors` can see. A **model-free** module both sides import is the fix, and note
  the drift it prevents is not cosmetic: a button offering an id the server has renamed fails
  with a 400 that reads like a permissions problem.
- **An object lookup on a request-supplied key is not safe because it is guarded.** Both `in`
  and object indexing walk the prototype chain, so `"toString"` and `"__proto__"` pass the
  check — and `ACTIONS["__proto__"]` returns `Object.prototype`, which is **truthy**, survives a
  `!target` test, and only fails later on a missing `.status`. **Safe by accident is not safe.**
  A `Map` has no prototype chain, so the lookup is total and the validator is the same object
  the server switches on.
- **A badge that appears on everything cannot be trusted where it matters.** Only
  `hold_and_alert` actually stops a contest settling, so only those rounds are badged as holding
  settlement; the other two policies settle on time. Same reasoning makes the dialog report
  whether settlement was **actually** released rather than announcing it: a contest can be held
  by several rounds, and an operator told "settlement unblocked" while three others still hold
  it stops believing the message. Same class as **no aggregate may silently mean "trading
  only"** — a label that is right in general and wrong in the case you are looking at.

### Five things from the player play screen (5 September 2026)

The provider lifecycle is no longer API-only anywhere: a player can start, play and see the result
of a round by clicking. `13` s1.1a, 42 tests, 20 probes red on the expected test. It also closed a
**live** defect — no player screen read `gameType`, so a provider contest rendered the trading lobby
with a **"Start Trading"** button opening the forex workspace for a puzzle.

- **A GET must never consume an attempt, and a server component IS a GET.** An attempt is spent
 when a round is *created*, deliberately, so launching from a page's render burns a paying player's
 only attempt **because Next.js prefetches `<Link>` targets on hover**. No error, no log line, and
 the player never even clicked. This is why the play screen is a state machine rather than a
 redirect through the launch API — a later "simplification" back to launch-on-load reintroduces it,
 which is why the reason is in the file and not only in a test. The general form: **before putting
 a side effect in a server component, ask who else issues that GET** — browsers, prefetchers,
 pollers and crawlers all do.
- **Removing a field is stronger than remembering not to read it.** A `postMessage` is
 attacker-controlled by construction — the player has a developer console — so the frame message
 type has **no score field at all**, and a `finished` carrying `score`, `rawScore`, `points`,
 `prize` and `rank` parses to an object with two keys. Prove it **behaviourally** and assert the
 **whole** object: a field you did not think to check is the only way to notice one you did not
 expect, which is how the admin credential-rotation bug surfaced.
- **The same identifier appearing twice defeats a structural test, and the copies do different
 jobs.** `!expectedOrigin` guards the message listener in one place and the render in another. A
 probe that killed the **render** guard left the suite green, because the listener's copy satisfied
 a bare `/if \(!expectedOrigin\)/`. Fourth instance of the class after the fixed-character Edit
 guard, `canTransitionRound` and `MIN_REASON_LENGTH`: **count the occurrences and assert position
 within the construct**, never a bare identifier.
- **When two chapters disagree about a URL, check which one owns the subject before making them
 agree.** `09` E6 said `/play/[contestId]`; `13` s1 said `/competitions/[id]/play`. `13` is the
 routing chapter and was right. **The drift ran source → owner, the opposite direction to the one
 the paired-document table is written for** — so verifying the source is not verifying the chapter
 that owns the fact. Getting this wrong costs either a renamed live URL or two play routes for
 ever, and checking cost one search.
- **Two routes that redirect into each other must have conditions that are exact complements, and
 the failure is not a wrong screen.** `/trade` bounces a provider contest to `/play`; `/play`
 bounces a non-provider contest to `/trade`. Both derive from `isProviderContest`, so no loop is
 possible — but an added refusal that also redirects (a seatless player, say) gives an **infinite
 redirect**, which surfaces as a blank page or a browser error naming nothing. Pin it with a test
 that asserts the play route redirects on **exactly one** refusal.

**And the fourth collision of the `**/games/*` ESLint wildcard, on `components/games`**, after
`database/models/games`, `lib/services/games` and `components/admin/games`. One negation, exactly as
predicted. Written as its own exception rather than a blanket `!**/components/**`: a components
folder is precisely where somebody would eventually put a game module's own logic "to keep it near
the screen", and a blanket exemption lets that through silently.

**A deferral worth stating because a summary would round it up.** There is still **no CSP
`frame-src` allowlist**, and it is not an oversight — `next.config.ts` has no
Content-Security-Policy at all, so adding one is a platform-wide change that must also account for
the Nuvei payment flow and the tutorial embeds. More to the point **there is nothing to allowlist
yet**: `game_provider` stores `baseUrl`, the provider's *API* host, and the spec's own example puts
play on a different subdomain, so the play domain is a fact X4 collects from a real provider. Until
then the message origin is derived from the launch URL actually loaded, together with
`event.source === frame.contentWindow`, which no unrelated window can satisfy.

### Five things from R26, the admin cron that paid no Game Masters (5 September 2026)

R26 is closed: `apps/admin`'s `finalizeCompetition` calls `settleFeesAndGameMasters`, the same
shared stage the main app calls, and books the platform fee **net** of the commission. Pinned by
`__tests__/services/admin-finalize-gamemaster-parity.test.ts` (5 tests) and
`tools/probe-admin-gm-parity.ps1` (5 probes). **The fix is not retroactive and no backfill was
written.**

- **Distinguish a defect that is LOSING money from one that is merely reachable, because it
  changes what you build next.** Almost every finding in this programme has been latent — a
  provider contest that has never settled, a 0% package the admin UI would not let you create.
  This one was live: both apps register `checkAndFinalizeCompetitions` on an every-minute cron,
  so whether a referrer was paid depended on **which cron claimed the contest first**. There is
  no flag, no error and no log line on either branch. **Before scheduling a money defect, check
  whether anything actually reaches the broken path today** — and when it does, say so in the
  register, because "already occurred" and "would occur" get triaged differently.
- **When a payout is carved OUT of a fee, adding the payout means changing the fee's RECORD in
  the same edit.** The Game Masters' share comes out of the platform fee, so the figure booked as
  platform income must be net of it. Paying the referrer while still recording the gross fee
  counts the same credits twice in two different books — and it reviews as a correct fix, since
  the referral row that was missing is now there. The parity suite therefore asserts the **net**
  figure explicitly, not just the earnings row. General form: **a new money row is an incomplete
  fix until you have asked which existing row it was taken from.**
- **A probe that stays green is a question with three answers, and the third one appeared here:
  the guard is real but UNREACHABLE on this path.** After "weak test" and "wrong claim", this was
  a correct check in `distribute.ts` that a second finalize never reaches, because the admin path
  refuses at `status !== "active"` long before the referral stage runs. That is the X1 finding
  again — **the four finalize functions are not four copies of one function**; this one has no
  retry wrapper and no optimistic lock, so its idempotency lives somewhere different from the main
  app's. The probe was re-aimed at the guard that actually holds the property, and **the test's
  comment, which had asserted the wrong mechanism, was corrected rather than quietly deleted.**
- **Extracting shared code HIDES divergence from a size comparison, which is how this defect was
  found in the first place.** R26 was spotted because `competition-end.actions.ts` was 72 KB in
  the main app against 38 KB in admin, and a 34 KB gap between two supposed copies is worth
  reading. It is now **45 KB against 37 KB** — not because admin gained the logic, but because the
  main app shed ~27 KB into shared services. **The same heuristic applied to the same pair today
  raises nothing.** The identical shape is still unexamined in `challenge-finalize.actions.ts`
  (70 KB against 42 KB, its own copy of all three stages in each app). This argues for parity
  tests, not against extraction — and note `check:mirrors` compares **models**, so it has never
  had an opinion about either file.
- **Say that a fix is not retroactive, and say why the damage cannot be queried for.** Contests
  already finalized by the admin cron paid nothing and wrote **no `retained_gm_fee` row either**,
  so the affected set cannot be found by searching the ledger for retained rows — only by
  reconciling referred players' entry fees against earnings per contest. No backfill was written,
  deliberately: which contests to compensate is an owner decision, and a script that credits
  wallets from inferred history is an unreviewed money writer. **"Fixed" without "not
  retroactive" is the kind of summary that makes somebody stop looking.**

**And a fifth instance of the aside-verification rule, this time in three files at once.** The
`walletMap` passed from the prize stage to the fee stage was documented in both apps as mattering
"for correctness, not just query count" — supposedly so a Game Master who also won a prize had
their commission's `balanceBefore` computed after the prize. The concern is real; **the map is not
what answers it.** Both stages read the post-credit balance back from
`findOneAndUpdate({ new: true })`, and neither reads a balance out of the map at all — it decides
only whether a wallet must be created. A **control probe** passing an empty map moves identical
money and must stay green. Comments corrected in both copies, with the correction left visible.

**Two harness lessons, both of which had already produced a false result.** `Get-Content -Raw`
and `[File]::ReadAllText` disagree on Windows: PowerShell 5.1 decodes with the system ANSI
codepage, so **every emoji in the touched services came back as mojibake and was written back that
way** — probes passed, files were quietly mangled, and it surfaced two steps later as unexplained
typecheck errors. Pin UTF-8 **without a BOM** on the read *and* the write, and assert a lossless
round trip before probing. Separately, the harness judged probes by searching whole-suite output
for the expected test's **name**, which vitest prints for a passing test as readily as a failing
one, so every probe reported RED beside "failing tests: 0". Run the expected test alone with `-t`
and read the summary counts; run the suite again only to measure blast radius.

**And one self-inflicted lesson that cost ten minutes and looked exactly like a hang.** Piping the
harness to `Select-String` showed nothing for eleven minutes, and it was killed as hung. The script
reports through `Write-Host`, which writes to the **host, not the pipeline**, so the filter was
reading a nearly empty stream while the probes ran normally — they finish in about two minutes.
Redirect a child PowerShell process's output to a file and poll that. **Silence from a filtered
pipeline is not evidence of a stalled process**, and killing a probe harness mid-run is how an
injected defect gets committed.

### Five things from X6's provider contest editing (7 September 2026)

`12` s2.2 is code-complete: a provider contest is editable, the Edit link routes by game, and
the trading route's blind `Object.assign` is gone. 34 tests, **16 probes red on exactly the
expected test**. Nothing here is mirrored.

- **A control withheld because it would corrupt data is worth re-reading, because the reason
  it was withheld may be a live defect somewhere else.** Edit was kept from provider contests
  because `PUT /api/competitions/[id]` blind-assigns the trading form's body. That was true and
  it was a third of the story: every field on `Competition` was writable by any admin-token
  holder on **trading** contests too — `gameKey`, `status`, `prizePool`, `currentParticipants` —
  and had been since long before this programme. **The general form: a documented workaround
  describes a hazard, and the hazard is rarely scoped to the case that made you notice it.**
  Related and now the **fifth** instance: the route used `verifyAdminToken`, which is token
  validity, not section access, after Prerequisite A, the internal-secret fallbacks, the
  unprotected suspicion-score route and the provider admin routes.
- **An allow-list must REFUSE an unknown field, never drop it — and the never-editable list
  must be tested by WHICH refusal fired.** Dropping is tidier and it means the edit appears to
  save while doing nothing, which is this codebase's recurring failure mode. The subtler half:
  the probe removing `gameKey` from `NEVER_EDITABLE_FIELDS` **stayed green**, because `gameKey`
  is absent from the allow-list too and so still fell through to the unknown-field refusal,
  whose message *also* contains the word "gameKey". The assertion had to pin the specific error
  text, or a later edit adding a field to the allow-list silently removes its immutability.
  **Sixth "probe stayed green" instance, answer: weak test.**
- **Never look a request-supplied key up in an object.** `ALLOWED[key]` walks the prototype
  chain, so `"constructor"` is admitted — truthy, survives a `!allowed` test, and fails later
  somewhere unrelated. A `Set` has no prototype chain, so the check is total. **Second instance
  after the round-inspector action map**, so carry the rule not the cases. And the aside that
  came with it was **wrong and was corrected**: `for...in` does *not* admit inherited keys on a
  **JSON-parsed** body, because `Object.prototype`'s members are non-enumerable — the
  vulnerability was the lookup, not the enumeration, and running the probe is what showed it.
- **A freeze must key on PARTICIPANTS, not on status.** A `draft` with entrants is impossible
  today; an `upcoming` contest with twenty paid seats is the normal case, and a status-keyed
  freeze lets its entry fee change underneath them. Three tiers: nothing frozen at zero;
  `name`, `description` and a **raising** `maxParticipants` once anyone has entered; nothing at
  all once `finalizing`, `completed`, `cancelled` or `emergency_ended`. **`finalizing` is the
  one that matters** — a change landing then may or may not be counted depending purely on
  timing, the same reason X3 treats it as closed for late results.
- **An edit re-runs the pre-flight against the STORED record**, exactly as publishing does. A
  draft outlives the switches that made it valid: the title can be disabled, the provider
  disabled, the adapter uninstalled. Settings are re-validated against the **live**
  `configSchema` and the **coerced** values stored, so `"7"` from an HTML input never reaches
  the provider as a string. Related deviation, recorded rather than absorbed: `12` section 2
  permits a game-type change on a zero-participant draft and the build **refuses it at every
  point**, because `gameKey` is immutable and a draft is cheap to delete and recreate — the
  permission buys nothing and costs an immutability guarantee.

**And a probe-harness lesson: parameterise the harness on the SUITE, not just the test name.**
The 16th probe had to prove a guard pinned by a test in a *different* file — the flipped
withholding test. Run against the default suite it reported "no test ran", which reads exactly
like a broken harness rather than a missing guard. Sixth probing instance, and the same shape
as every other one: **a probe aimed at the wrong place is indistinguishable from a test that
does not work.**

**One more on flipping tests.** The withholding test was flipped rather than deleted, and the
mutation chosen for it matters: deleting one destination is caught by any test that mentions
the other, so the probe **swaps** them. A test asserting only that the game editor is linked is
green on a swap, and a swap is exactly what sends a provider contest to the trading form.

### Five things from X6's live-contest controls (7 September 2026)

The lifecycle controls are game-aware and, more to the point, authorized. 63 tests, 31 probes red,
admin typecheck at the 223 baseline exactly. **R40** and **R41** closed.

- **The route with NO guard is not found by reading the ones with weak guards.** Six of the seven
  lifecycle routes authenticated on admin-at-all rather than section access, which is bad and
  reviewable. The seventh — `POST /api/finalize-old-competitions` — had **nothing**, and every
  neighbour having *something* is precisely why reading through them would have gone straight
  past it. It surfaced from **counting exported handlers against guards**, which is a different
  activity from reading each route and which also catches the subtler shape: a file whose `POST`
  is guarded and whose `GET` is not passes any mention-based check while leaving a mutation open.
  Second unauthenticated route after Prerequisite A, so carry the class. And **a route with no
  guard has no attribution**, so "did this ever happen" is unanswerable afterwards — state that
  rather than implying the absence of evidence is reassuring.
- **A capability the platform already has does not extend to a new game by itself, and the way it
  fails is silence.** `isPaused` was not missing, undocumented or broken — `round-launch.service.ts`
  simply never read it, so an operator pausing a provider contest got a success toast, a PAUSED
  banner and a notification to every participant while players carried on. Worse than a dead
  control, because `IncidentsSection.tsx` pauses a contest when an incident is raised: the moment
  they most need play to stop is the moment they were most confidently told it had. The rule:
  **when adding a game, enumerate the operator controls that already exist and ask which code
  path enforces each one** — not whether the field is set. Same family as the trading-shaped
  services in `matchmaking.service.ts`, one layer further out.
- **A guard that compensates for a pause has to extend the field that GATES play, and the field
  named "end" is not it.** Resume extended `endTime`. `createRound` gates on `playWindowEnd` and
  the launch service on `playWindowStart`; `endTime` gates neither, so the contest ran longer
  while the window players actually play inside stayed exactly as short — a two-hour pause simply
  consumed two hours of playing time, and it reads as correct because of the field's name. The
  second half is as important: shift `playWindowStart` **only while it is still future**, because
  moving a window that has already opened re-closes it and refuses play that was legitimately
  available a moment earlier.
- **A wording pass that swaps the noun is worse than leaving the old wording, because it reads as
  though somebody checked.** The operator's control panel said seven trading-shaped things, the
  worst being "All positions will be closed at current prices" directly above the emergency-cancel
  confirm button on a contest with no positions. The consequence lists genuinely **differ** —
  pausing a provider contest stops rounds being started *or resumed*; emergency-cancelling one
  voids live rounds instead of closing positions and recording P&L — so they live in a shared
  **model-free** module (the panel is `"use client"`, the same constraint behind
  `components/games/play-state.ts` and the round-resolution action list, both of which were first
  written as a second copy). And **the flag is derived server-side by `hasProviderGameLabel` and
  passed in**: what an operator is told about a money-adjacent action must not be decidable in the
  browser, or the admin app has two answers to "is this a provider contest" with the untested one
  in front of the operator.
- **Three green probes, one shape: the injected defect satisfied the assertion with a DIFFERENT
  string.** Restoring the real defect verbatim passed a guard written against the phrase "open
  positions", because the dialog says "All positions". Deleting the mid-round resume line passed a
  bare `/resum/i`, because the next item says "when resumed". Replacing the pause list's mention
  of positions passed an assertion over the whole copy object, because the emergency list still
  said "positions". Two rules: **a vocabulary guard must match the WORD, never a phrase copied
  out of one of the sites it polices**, and **a per-list claim must be asserted per list**, since
  one list covering for another is indistinguishable from the guard working.

**And a fourth green probe was the third cause — the guard is real but unreachable.** The
transition check in the cleanup loop can never fire: `LIVE_ROUND_STATUSES` is `pending`/`launched`
and `ROUND_TRANSITIONS` permits `voided` from both, so the query filter has already guaranteed a
legal move. It is **kept as a tripwire** for the day `unresolved` joins that list, and the comment
says so rather than claiming a fix — an overstated comment is a wrong fact, the same duty as
correcting R7 and R31 downward. The probe and the test were re-aimed at the query filter, which is
where the property actually lives.

**Two structural-test lessons, both from getting it wrong here.** **Locate the construct; do not
scan towards it.** `<ul[^>]*>[\s\S]*?copy\.X\.map` matches **leftmost-first**, so it opened at an
unrelated `<ul>` several hundred characters earlier and swallowed four legitimate literal items —
the guard then failed on *correct* code, which is the fastest way to have it deleted. Slice by
index instead. Same class as the fixed-character Edit guard that began mid-identifier. And
**assert that a slice found the right thing**: `indexOf("return (")` matched
`return () => clearInterval(interval)` inside an effect and sliced away the markup entirely; only
a length assertion caught it, because a test examining nothing passes.

**One claim was wrong rather than weak, and the correction is narrower than the original.** "No
trading wording anywhere in the panel" is false and must not be restored: the emergency toast
keeps "N positions closed" in its **trading** branch, because an operator running a trading
contest still needs to be told what happened to their positions. The honest claim is **no
*unconditional* trading wording**, asserted over the JSX with the branched strings in the handlers.

### Five things from R42, the admin cron that would not settle a provider contest (7 September 2026)

R42 is closed: `apps/admin`'s `finalizeCompetition` now dispatches provider contests to
`finalizeProviderCompetition`, the same way the main app has since X5. 3 tests appended to
`__tests__/services/admin-finalize-gamemaster-parity.test.ts`, 4 probes in
`tools/probe-admin-provider-dispatch.ps1`, all red with exactly 1 failure each. **Latent and
nothing backfilled** — no provider contest has settled in production.

- **A mirrored file is not a reachable one, and this is the case that shows `check:mirrors`
  being correctly silent.** `provider-finalize.ts` and `provider-settlement.service.ts` were
  copied into `apps/admin` during X5 **and imported by nothing** for three days. The two copies
  agreed; only the call site was absent. The guard compares models and files and has never had
  an opinion about callers — so **a green `check:mirrors` is not evidence that mirrored code
  runs**, which is the same class as the older rule that it is not evidence a field is stored.
- **A heuristic that found the last defect is not evidence about the next one.** R26 surfaced
  from a 34 KB size gap between these same two files. That gap is now 8 KB, because the main app
  shed code into shared services, and a six-line dispatch does not move it at all. The
  conclusion is to extend the parity suite rather than re-measure — and the real gap was the
  fixture: **every test in it seeded a trading contest**, so a provider-shaped one had never
  been handed to either finalizer. Same shape as the score-seam finding, where the suites seeded
  the value under test.
- **When two guards cover each other, neither can be probed alone — and saying so beats
  shipping a green probe.** Removing either game gate left the suite green, because the other
  refuses an unknown label too; both had to go in one edit, by stubbing the shared import. The
  main app's answer to this situation — assert `updatedAt` never moved — **does not transfer**,
  because this path takes no optimistic lock, so there is no `finalizing` state to strand a
  contest in and an aborted transaction is indistinguishable from a never-opened one. The probe
  file **records the second gate as unprobed with the reason**, rather than carrying a fifth
  probe that reports green and teaches the next reader that the gate is decoration.
- **A terminal status is not evidence of a settlement.** A fixture bug proved it for free: the
  seeded participants passed `competitionId` as an ObjectId where the schema declares `String`,
  and the raw driver does no casting, so settlement matched nothing. It did not crash — it
  logged `Found 0 participants`, booked the whole pool as an unclaimed pool, recorded a platform
  fee, marked the contest `completed` and **returned success.** Only the prize count disagreed.
  **Assert the money separately from the status**, because the status assertion passes either way.
- **Dispatch ordering is a constraint from the callee, not a style choice.** It sits before
  `startSession()` because `finalizeProviderCompetition` opens its own session and takes its own
  lock, so dispatching after nests a transaction inside one the caller owns. And the gate left
  behind inside the transaction had a comment claiming to *be* the dispatch — true when this app
  had no provider path, wrong the moment one arrived. **Correct such a comment in place rather
  than adjusting its tense**, so the next reader knows it was once believed.

**And the finding about how it was found, which is the sixth instance of a rule already carried.**
Not by planned work: a mapping subagent asserted it in passing and the claim was checked before
being accepted. After `challengeId`, the R7 severity, `billsPerRound`, the `participant.score`
comment and the `walletMap` comment, this is the first where the unverified aside came from
**another agent** rather than from our own documentation. The rule generalises unchanged: **an
unverified claim is a claim whoever wrote it**, and other agents' reports get the same suspicion
as a comment in a file.

### Five things from the analytics-by-provider slice (7 September 2026)

`12` s5.1a is code-complete: the analytics screen is game-aware, two financial breakdowns were
added, and Game Performance exists. 50 tests, 39 probes red on exactly the expected test.

- **Opening a screen to generalise it is the fifth time that has been a better bug-finding
  instrument than looking for bugs, and this time THREE of the four defects were not about
  games.** The route authenticated on admin-at-all; **"Prize %" had never worked for any game
  since the screen was written**, because nothing has ever written `metadata.percentage`, so it
  read `0%` beside every winner of every competition ever settled; and every headline card was
  captioned as a total while covering the last 50 contests. Only "Final P&L" was the game gap.
  The general form: **a screen written when there was one case is not wrong only in the ways
  the second case makes obvious** — the other faults were reachable the whole time and nobody
  had cause to read the file.
- **A caption is a claim, and correcting it is not the same job as correcting the number.** The
  totals really do cover 50 contests, so the tempting fix is to widen the window. That is a
  behaviour change to every figure on the screen, and **making it in the same edit as a
  labelling fix destroys the only evidence the labelling fix was safe** — the same reasoning
  that kept the Game Master `||` verbatim while settlement was extracted. Label it, send the
  limit from the server so the caption cannot drift from the query, and record the arithmetic
  as outstanding.
- **Group by the immutable key, never by the display name, and the failure is silent in the
  most reassuring direction.** `gameKey` is what every historical figure joins on; a display
  name is catalogue content an operator can edit. Group by the name and **renaming a title
  splits one game's revenue into two rows that each look complete**, with no error and totals
  that still add up. Same family as `gameKey` being immutable and as R29 retiring rows rather
  than deleting them — and the corollary is that the label's fallback chain must end at the
  code and then the key, **never at "Unknown"**, because a row captioned "Unknown game" holding
  real revenue cannot be investigated.
- **A screen's grant decides what may be ON it.** Game Performance omits money not for layout
  reasons but because it is granted by a games section while revenue is granted by `analytics`
  and `financial` — so a revenue figure there is a **silent widening of who can read the
  platform's earnings**, and it reviews as a helpful addition. Two things about pinning it:
  assert **the fields the component reads off its data**, not the word, because the description
  deliberately names fee revenue in order to signpost the screen that carries it and a
  word-check would forbid the signpost; and **the positive half is not decoration** — a screen
  that omits money without saying where money lives teaches an operator the figures do not
  exist.
- **A probe that changes no observable is the fourth cause of a green probe, after weak test,
  wrong claim and unreachable guard.** A probe meant to drop trading from the by-provider
  summary changed only the **group key**, leaving the badge and every figure identical, so with
  one trading contest present the output was byte-for-byte the same and the probe reported the
  guard absent. **It is indistinguishable from a test that does not work**, and the fix is to
  ask what the injected defect would look like on screen before believing either outcome.

**And two data-shape traps that each produced a plausible number rather than an error.** The
participation funnel was first scoped to `gameKey` alone, so the "never played" shortfall was
every seat the title had ever sold measured against one window's players — a figure that grows
for ever and always looks bad. And the played set was taken from all ranked rounds, which
includes **challenges**, against a competition-seat denominator: wrong on any game and
**negative** on a busy one. Both now come from competition rounds in the window, and the
difference is a **set difference rather than a subtraction of two counts**, because the sets
come from different collections and a plain subtraction silently improves whenever anyone
appears in the round set who is not a seat. Third instance of the ObjectId/String boundary
after the R42 fixture and the score seam: **`game_round.contestId` is an ObjectId,
`competition_participant.competitionId` is declared `String`, the raw driver does no casting,
and an unconverted `$in` reports every entrant as having played while logging nothing.**

### Five things from the Game Master creation gate (7 September 2026)

`19` s3.2a is code-complete: `lib/services/gamemaster/game-permissions.ts` (mirrored) is the one
answer to "which games may this Game Master run", `allowedGameTypes` is declared on the
subscription model (main app only - the admin app reaches that collection with the raw driver) and
on both copies of the package model, both creation routes are rewritten onto it, and **R47** is
closed. 59 tests, 19 probes red on exactly the expected test. **The permission half only** — a
Game Master still cannot construct a provider contest.

- **Permission and CAPABILITY are two questions, and answering only the first makes the grant
  destructive.** Widening `allowedGameTypes` to `provider` is necessary and not sufficient: the
  routes can build a trading contest and nothing else, so the grant alone produces a contest
  stamped `gameKey: "provider"` rather than `provider:<providerKey>:<gameCode>` — and `gameKey`
  is **immutable**, so that is unrecoverable data rather than a wrong screen. The refusal names
  the missing capability instead. **The general form: before making a permission configurable,
  check that every value it can now take is one the code path can actually honour**, and refuse
  the rest explicitly. Same family as the market-hours gate failing closed on an unknown game.
- **A refusal must name WHO refused, or it sends the user somewhere that cannot help them.**
  `resolveCreationLimits` reports `creationDecidedBy` — override, package, cached limits or
  default — and that is not diagnostics: "your package does not allow competition creation" is
  actively misleading when an administrator denied it by hand, and it sends a paying partner to
  buy an upgrade that changes nothing. The same fact fixed the badge, which read the cached flag
  under a tooltip crediting the package, so an explicit deny rendered **green** while every
  create was refused. And the fix belongs in the **route**, with a test asserting the component
  does **not** re-derive precedence — a second copy of a precedence rule in the browser is the
  "one rule, two copies" shape, and the negative assertion is the load-bearing half because
  importing a resolved value is trivially satisfied by a component that recomputes it anyway.
- **An empty stored array is a missing value, not an empty set — and taking it literally locks
  people out of what they paid for.** `resolveAllowedGameTypes` treats `[]` as the default,
  because an empty array is what a bad edit, a half-run migration or a form submitting no
  checkboxes leaves behind. This is the *opposite* reading to `entryBlockThreshold`'s "a stored
  value and an absent one are different facts", and the difference is which way the failure
  falls: there, a stored value was being over-trusted; here, a *shape* that only ever arises by
  accident would be. **Decide by asking whether any legitimate writer can produce the empty
  case** — nothing offers "no games", so nothing means it.
- **An override that AGREES with the current rule is still worth storing, which is why there are
  two directions and not one toggle.** Force-on and force-off both survive the package being
  edited underneath them; a single toggle can only ever express "the opposite of whatever applies
  right now", so the moment a tier changes, the recorded decision silently becomes the wrong one.
  Related, and the reason the dead control was implemented rather than deleted on the
  `shouldBlockEntry` precedent: it is the **only per-Game-Master control**, so without it the
  alternatives are changing a tier for everyone on it, or suspending a subscription and stopping
  earnings the partner is contractually owed. **Deleting a dead control is right when a working
  one exists elsewhere; here nothing else could express the decision.**
- **Two routes that do the same job disagree about every rule, not just the one you came for.**
  The work set out to add a games list. It found that the main route floored `minParticipants` at
  **1** (a paid single-player contest, against a hard constraint) while the admin route hardcoded
  2 but read only the cached `subscription.limits` and **never checked `canCreateCompetitions` at
  all**, so a Game Master whose package had withdrawn creation could still create through it.
  **Sixth instance of the counting rule**, in its "count the copies, then diff their rules" form —
  after four entry paths, ten finalize sites, six raw inserts, seven lifecycle routes and seven
  writers of the referral rate. And `check:mirrors` compares models, so the guarantee for the new
  shared module is a **byte-for-byte test**, which caught the mirror genuinely stale mid-build
  before the admin typecheck did.

**Two things about how R47 was found, both of which are techniques rather than facts.**
`POST /api/gamemasters/sync-referrals` had **no authentication on either handler** while all four
siblings required section access, and it was found by **counting exported handlers against
guards** — the same method as R40, and the only one that works here, because every neighbour
having a guard is precisely what sends a reader past the file that has none. **State such an
exposure in both directions:** it could not redirect commission to a caller, but it could apply an
attribution change an operator had deliberately withheld and drive an unbounded pass over every
active referral, and the GET handed out real user ids and names. **There is no way to know whether
it was ever called**, so do not let the absence of evidence read as reassurance. Separately, the
`update_limits` mass assignment was aggravated by the route updating with the **raw driver**, so
the schema's own `min: 2` never applied on that path — **a validation rule is only as strong as
the weakest writer**, and the allow-list is a `Set` so a request-supplied `"constructor"` cannot
pass a lookup that walks the prototype chain (third instance, after the round-inspector action map
and the contest-edit field list).

**And a probe came back green for the third reason, which is now recorded twice: no test existed
at all.** Restoring the blind spread left `resolveCreationLimits` in the file, so the badge test
the probe had been aimed at stayed satisfied while the mass assignment was wide open. **Naming the
expected failing test is what exposed it**; a bare "did the suite go red" would have recorded a
passing probe. A second probe now aims at the positive assertion too, because a validator that is
called and then ignored is green on the negative one.

## The rule

**Documentation is part of the deliverable, not a follow-up task.** A phase is not
finished until the documents match what was actually built.

On every change during implementation:

1. **Update `PROGRESS.md`** in the relevant folder — status table, work log entry,
   decisions, open questions. This is the file a new chat reads first.
2. **Update the affected chapter(s).** If the data model changed, `04-data-model.md`
   changes. If a flow changed, the flow document changes.
3. **Update the HTML versions**, which are generated from the same content and drift
   silently otherwise:
   - `New games plan/ChartVolt-New-Games-Plan.html` — internal
   - `New games plan/ChartVolt-New-Games-Plan-Partners.html` — partner-facing
   - `External game plans/ChartVolt-External-Games-Plan.html` — internal
   - `External game plans/ChartVolt-Game-API-Requirements.html` — sent to providers
4. **Record deviations explicitly.** If the build differed from the plan, say so in the
   work log with the reason. Never silently rewrite the plan to match the code.
5. **Check whether docs outside these folders are affected** — `README.md`,
   `.cursor/rules/*.mdc`, `deploy/README.md`, the admin wiki
   (`apps/admin/components/admin/AdminWikiSection.tsx`), and
   `legal/ChartVolt-Regulatory-Defence-Pack.html` if anything touches the
   skill-versus-chance argument or the money flow.

